const STUDIO_SPEC = `Context: ${U}/studio is a private Node dashboard (deps: marked, yaml) deployed to Railway with \`railway up\` from ${U} (studio/README.md, package.json, .railwayignore, railway.json; studio/check.mjs = test suite). A previous, INTERRUPTED run started a volume-based media store (MEDIA_DIR + a token PUT upload API, studio/lib/media.mjs, tools/motion/sync-media.mjs) — its last fix pass may be half-applied. We are switching to a Railway STORAGE BUCKET instead (lighter: video bytes never go through the studio). The studio service now has env BUCKET_NAME, BUCKET_ENDPOINT (https://t3.storageapi.dev, virtual-hosted style), BUCKET_REGION, BUCKET_ACCESS_KEY_ID, BUCKET_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY (S3-compatible, Tigris). Railway buckets are private; serve via presigned URLs. Bucket egress is free.

BUILD:
1. studio/lib/s3.mjs — dependency-free AWS SigV4 (node:crypto): presignGet(key, {expires=3600, download filename?}) (adds response-content-disposition=attachment when downloading), listObjects(prefix) (ListObjectsV2 with continuation), putObject(key, fileStream|buffer, {contentType, sha256?}) using UNSIGNED-PAYLOAD with Content-Length (stream from disk, never buffer whole files; S3 single PUT ≤ 5 GB is fine), headObject(key), deleteObject(key). Virtual-hosted URL https://<bucket>.<endpoint host>/<key>; URI-encode keys correctly (spaces, unicode). Unit-test the signer against AWS's published SigV4 test vector (presigned GET example from the S3 docs) in check.mjs.
2. Media served from the bucket: object keys = projects/<project>/outputs/<file>. /media/<project>/<file> → after the normal auth, 302 to a presigned GET (1 hour); ?download=1 → presigned with attachment disposition. Locally (no BUCKET_* env) keep serving files from disk exactly as today. Gallery/listing = local files when present, else bucket listing (cache the listing ~60 s). On the bucket we store BOTH masters (<name>.mp4) and previews (<name>.preview.mp4): play the preview in-page, download the master.
3. Remove the volume/MEDIA_DIR upload API (PUT/DELETE /api/media…) and its tests; keep a read-only GET /api/media/<project> list (Basic or Bearer) backed by the bucket. Remove MEDIA_DIR code paths cleanly.
4. NEW /videos page (add to the nav): one card per project that has videos, sorted by publish date, NZ/Global filter, same design language as the rest of the studio. Each card: the player (preview) on the LEFT with every version listed under it (aspect, ▶ to switch, ⬇ Download full quality via ?download=1); on the RIGHT that project's own voice script from vo-script.json: one row per line — big "say this" text (QSME shown as QSME, i.e. replace Q-S-me), a short "how" (first sentence of delivery, production timings stripped), a "details" toggle (on-screen text, full direction, file name <id>.m4a, must fit in max_s), and a per-line Copy button; plus Copy all / ⬇ .txt. While the video plays, the current line highlights; clicking a line's time seeks the video. Also show the post caption & hashtags (from post.md) in a collapsible block with Copy. It must work on a phone (stacked) and desktop (side by side). Reference implementation of the layout and behaviour: ${U}/tools/videos_page.py (generates ${U}/videos.html) — port it to the studio's server-rendered views + small vanilla JS (CSP-safe: no inline handlers if the CSP forbids them).
5. Sync tool ${U}/tools/motion/sync-media.mjs (local): for each projects/*/outputs/ top-level file (masters *.mp4, previews *.preview.mp4, png/jpg/webp/gif stills, mp3/m4a/wav) compare with the bucket (ListObjectsV2 size + ETag or a sha256 in x-amz-meta-sha256) and PUT only new/changed, with the right Content-Type; --prune deletes bucket objects with no local file. Credentials: read at runtime from \`railway variables --service studio --kv\` run in ${U} (parse BUCKET_*), NEVER print them, never write them to disk. Reuse studio/lib/s3.mjs (import from ../../studio/lib/s3.mjs). Print a short summary (uploaded/skipped/MB).
6. package.json "publish" = node tools/motion/previews.mjs → synced.txt → railway up --detach → node tools/motion/sync-media.mjs. .railwayignore must keep ALL media out of the code upload (verify with git check-ignore in a throwaway repo; don't let placeholder files overwrite that repo's .gitignore).
7. Tests (check.mjs, keep the rest green): signer test vector; /media redirects (302 to a URL on the bucket host with X-Amz-Signature) when BUCKET env is set (use fake creds and a fake endpoint — don't hit the network), download disposition, auth still required for /media and /videos, /videos renders cards + per-line copy buttons from a fixture project, traversal attempts on /media never produce a presigned URL for a key outside projects/<p>/outputs/. Run npm run check until green. Update studio/README.md and ${U}/tools/motion/README.md (publish flow).
Rules: don't touch content folders (ideas/, ventures/, projects/ content, research/, log/, CLAUDE.md). Don't deploy, don't run railway up, don't print secrets. You MAY run \`railway variables --service studio --kv\` only inside the sync tool's code path; for a live smoke test of the sync tool, run it with --dry-run (list + compare, no uploads) and report the summary.`
const ISS = { type: 'object', properties: { issues: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'object', properties: { severity: { type: 'string', enum: ['critical', 'high', 'medium', 'low'] }, where: { type: 'string' }, problem: { type: 'string' }, fix: { type: 'string' } }, required: ['severity', 'where', 'problem', 'fix'] } }, summary: { type: 'string' } }, required: ['issues', 'summary'] }
const studioChain = async () => {
  const built = await agent(`You are a careful senior Node engineer.\n${STUDIO_SPEC}\nReturn a concise report: files changed, tests, how publish works now, anything not done.`, { label: 'studio:bucket+videos', phase: 'Studio' })
  const rv = await agent(`You are a security + UX reviewer. Review the bucket media + /videos page + sync tool in ${U}/studio, ${U}/tools/motion/sync-media.mjs, package.json, .railwayignore against this spec:\n${STUDIO_SPEC}\nBuilder report:\n${built}\nActually run things: npm run check; start the server locally (STUDIO_PASSWORD=test, fake BUCKET_* env with a fake endpoint) and probe /media (auth, traversal, encoded paths, ?download), /videos (desktop + phone width markup, per-line copy, highlight JS, CSP compliance); verify the SigV4 code against the AWS test vector yourself; run the sync tool with --dry-run and make sure it never prints a credential. Do NOT modify files. Return issues with exact fixes.`, { label: 'studio:review', phase: 'Studio', schema: ISS })
  const fixed = await agent(`Apply these review findings to the studio bucket/videos/sync work (${U}/studio, ${U}/tools/motion/sync-media.mjs, package.json, .railwayignore). Fix all critical/high/medium, low if cheap; add a test per security fix; npm run check until green. Same rules: no deploy, no secrets printed, don't touch content.\nFINDINGS: ${JSON.stringify(rv)}\nBUILD REPORT: ${built}`, { label: 'studio:fix', phase: 'Studio' })
  return { built, review: rv, fixed }
}

const prog = {"calendars":{"nz":{"weeks":[{"week":2,"starts":"2026-10-12","theme":"Will it stack up? What an Auckland section would really take (feasibility)","posts":[{"date":"2026-10-13","time_nzt":"19:30","week":2,"idea_id":"nz-granny-flat-for-mum","title":"Granny flat for Mum (Flat 2 gets a vote)","existing_project":"","format":"short","kind":"situation-demo (funny)","length_s":33,"aspect":"9:16","platforms":["Instagram Reels","TikTok","Facebook Reels","YouTube Shorts"],"hook_text":"Granny flat for Mum. 'It's easy now, the rules changed.'","concept":"An Auckland family wants a 60 m² granny flat out the back so Mum can move closer. They've heard the new rules make it easy. Their place is Flat 1 on a cross-lease. 0-3s hook: the gf-3-placed b-roll (a crane lowering the unit; the workers are small and unidentifiable; labelled Illustrative), then a hard cut to a red REQUIRED stamp. Never use gf-1-idea: its two AI-generated people stand front and centre and would read as the family. 3-10s: 'What this site would require' on the row 'Granny flat without consent — …can often be added without resource consent… where the site meets the conditions'. A highlighter goes over 'can often' and 'where the site meets the conditions'. Caption: '“Can often.” Read the conditions.' On-screen text never says 'no consent needed' (red list); the product's own conditional row does the talking. 10-18s: tap the 'Required' filter and the list collapses to the must-haves; zoom on 'Other cross-lease owners' consent — On a cross-lease the other flat owners must consent to any change of footprint…' and 'because: Cross-lease title'. 18-24s: a kinetic stick-figure sketch (no AI people) pans to Flat 2, labelled '(have only ever discussed bins)'. Caption: 'Flat 2 gets a say. We've only ever talked about bins.' 24-29s: the 'LIM & Record of Title — Order a LIM and the title before committing…' row. Caption: 'Next: the LIM, the title, and a cuppa with next door.' Small: 'Read from the legal description: a clue, not a title search.' 29-33s end card: 'See what your Auckland section would require. Free account. qsme.co.nz'. The Mel VO script for later lands on 'Might bake something.' The joke is the cross-lease situation and our own awkwardness, never the neighbours. This version drops the original zone-card beat so it doesn't repeat Sunday's long-form, and takes the 'bins' line from Just a deck.","features":["“What this site would require” checklist"],"owner_recordings_needed":["SESSION A (finish by Fri 9 Oct): signed-in Site research on an Auckland cross-lease residential section (the owner's own, or a friend's with written consent). Record the 'What this site would require' card ONLY, with no address bar, map, parcel or legal description visible. Shot 1: slow scroll on the 'Granny flat without consent…' row. Shot 2: tapping the 'Required' filter and landing on 'Other cross-lease owners' consent' with 'because: Cross-lease title'. Shot 3: the 'LIM & Record of Title' row. If no single site shows both the granny-flat and cross-lease rows, record two sites and caption 'Two Auckland sites', or fall back to the Just a deck cut, which only needs the cross-lease row."],"why_this_slot":"This is week 2's funny short, and it opens the feasibility week on the hottest housing topic: granny flats under the new national rules, with families planning summer builds in spring. Tuesday 7:30pm reaches homeowners and families at their best weeknight scroll on IG and FB. It's the first signed-in site-research video, made possible by the week-2 owner session, and its feature (the requirements checklist) is deliberately left out of Sunday's long-form."},{"date":"2026-10-15","time_nzt":"19:30","week":2,"idea_id":"nz-walk-away-number","title":"The walk-away number","existing_project":"","format":"short","kind":"situation-demo (relatable)","length_s":32,"aspect":"9:16","platforms":["Instagram Reels","TikTok","Facebook Reels","YouTube Shorts"],"hook_text":"Night before the auction. Our budget: a vibe.","concept":"The night before an Auckland auction, a first-time small developer's 'budget' is a number she said out loud in the car. 0-3s hook: kinetic text over the blurred green box (optional owner-filmed, hands-only laptop on a kitchen table at night). Text: 'Night before the auction. Our budget: a vibe.' 3-9s: at the laptop (a desktop recording, never the phone app), the 'Rough feasibility — Your numbers, our arithmetic.' card. Typing an illustrative Land price and Sale price per home flips Profit and 'Margin on cost' from '—' to figures. Every money frame is tagged 'Illustrative numbers'. 9-15s: raise 'Build cost per m² — edit to your builder's rate' until the margin goes red under 20%. Caption: 'Builder's real rate in. Margin goes red.' VO: 'Ouch.' 15-21s: hold on the green box 'What you could pay for the land to keep a 20% margin:' while a marker writes 'WALK-AWAY NUMBER' on a sticky note. Caption: 'That's the paddle limit.' 21-26s: next morning, 9:40am in the car park: the dashboard clock and the sticky note stuck to a plain card on the passenger seat (owner-filmed, or a kinetic fallback). Caption: 'Going once…' and the paddle stays down. Caption: 'Bid with a number, not a feeling.' 26-32s: a small-print card 'Rough: no GST, tax, holding costs or agent fees. Not financial advice. Run it past your builder, QS and bank.', then the end card 'Run your numbers before the room does. Site research: free account, Auckland first. qsme.co.nz'. Ships silent with a Mel VO script for later. The joke is auction FOMO, never bidders or agents. This merges in nz-auction-eve's 'night before' framing, 'budget is a vibe' hook and sticky note. The numbers are run at the laptop the night before, not in the car, because the phone Site search screen is merged but not confirmed in the store app; the video never implies the calculator runs in the iPhone app. The calculator's defaults are editable starting points, not QSME cost data.","features":["Rough feasibility calculator (“Your numbers, our arithmetic.”)"],"owner_recordings_needed":["SESSION A (finish by Fri 9 Oct): signed-in Site research on desktop, on an Auckland commercial site or the owner's own property, with no address bar. Shot 1: the Rough feasibility card with Homes prefilled. Shot 2: typing an illustrative land price and sale price per home until Profit and 'Margin on cost' appear. Shot 3: raising 'Build cost per m² — edit to your builder's rate' until Margin turns red under 20%. Shot 4: holding on the green 'What you could pay for the land to keep a 20% margin:' box.","OPTIONAL owner-filmed 3s: from the driver's seat in any car park, the dashboard clock reading about 9:40 and a plain card with a sticky note on the passenger seat. No faces, number plates or auction-house signage.","OPTIONAL owner-filmed, hands only: a laptop on a kitchen table at night (screen dark or showing the demo account), for the hook."],"why_this_slot":"Thursday 7:30pm lands the night-before story before the weekend's on-site auctions and is easy to save for Saturday morning, in the peak of Auckland's spring auction season. It's a different feature from Tuesday's (the calculator, not the checklist) and from Sunday's long-form, which only points to it. It comes from the same week-2 owner session as the other two posts."},{"date":"2026-10-18","time_nzt":"19:00","week":2,"idea_id":"nz-address-to-site-report","title":"Three sections, one planner: from an address to a site report","existing_project":"","format":"long","kind":"long-form walkthrough (deadpan)","length_s":150,"aspect":"16:9 (+9:16 chapter cut-downs)","platforms":["YouTube","Facebook","YouTube Shorts (chapter cuts)"],"hook_text":"It said 'Strong development potential.' Then I scrolled.","concept":"A first-time small developer has three Auckland sections on her shortlist and one planner she pays by the hour on Monday. Which address gets the meeting? The cold open (0:00-0:12) takes over from 'Strong. Then I scrolled.': the green STRONG pill, a scroll slam, then a red Constraints row 'Designation · Road Widening (Auckland Transport)', underlined in marker. 0:12-0:25: a sticky note with three fictional street names. 0:25-0:50: Site research loads ('Researching the site — boundary, zoning, overlays, services, hazards & history…'; crop the page headline 'Type any New Zealand address…' and never voice it), the parcel shows in cadastre yellow, then 'What could go here' (Without / With a resource consent), the standards grid and the Zoning layer. 0:50-1:15: 'Planning overlays & controls' grouped into Constraints / Design around / Opportunities (designation, Volcanic viewshaft, Height Variation Control), then pipes on the map with the dashed 'Distance to boundary' line and the beforeUdig note. Caption: 'Pipe positions approximate.' 1:15-1:35: the Development potential verdict and chips. Caption: '“Indicatively.” Every time.' A one-line pointer covers what the site would require and the rough numbers ('see this week's shorts'), with no demo. 1:35-2:00: 'Open Pro view': layer chips, tab pills, and the Title tab showing the owner count the site really returns (e.g. '1 owner on the title') with the locked 'Available with a data licence' pill (never mocked; title number and legal description blurred), then flicking between the three saved sites. 2:00-2:15: 'One pager' print preview (address and title block blurred), Save as PDF, and the printed page landing on a desk. 2:15-2:30 close: 'Indicative only… Confirm with a planner or the council.' VO: 'It even tells you to ring the planner. Fair enough.' CTA: 'Start with your address. Site research is a free account at qsme.co.nz. Full depth: Auckland.' Captions say Auckland only throughout. It's rule-based public data, not AI; 'Pro' is a view, not a plan; it's not a LIM, valuation or advice. Captions are burned in, with a deadpan Mel or QS VO script for later.","features":["Site research page (web, free account)","Zone-first zoning: “What could go here” and the coloured zoning map","Planning overlays & controls on the parcel (Auckland)","Services & hazards snapshot (Auckland): pipes on the map with 'Distance to boundary'","Development potential verdict and indicative yield","Site research — Pro view (full map, layers, seven tabs, title facts)","One-page site report (print or save as PDF)","Saved sites, recent searches and research kept with the project"],"owner_recordings_needed":["SESSION A (finish by Fri 9 Oct): desktop 1920x1080, signed in, on three Auckland addresses that are commercial or the owner's own. Use the ~209 m² Mount Eden Road Mixed Use test site only if it's confirmed commercial. Crop or blur every address bar, the page headline and map labels.","Shot A: Site research loading, the green progress line and the parcel in cadastre yellow.","Shot B: the 'What could go here' card and standards grid. Shot C: the Zoning layer toggle washing the street in zone colours.","Shot D: the Development potential card with the STRONG pill and +/− chips (for the cold open and chapter 4).","Shot E: scrolling to 'Planning overlays & controls': the red Designation row, amber Volcanic viewshaft, green Height Variation Control.","Shot F: the services map with pipes and the dashed 'Distance to boundary' line.","Shot G: 'Open Pro view': layer chips, tab pills, the Title tab with its real owner count and the locked data-licence pill; blur the title number, legal description and anything naming the property.","Shot H: switching between the three saved sites. Shot I: 'One pager' print preview and Save as PDF, with the address and title block blurred. Shot J: the grey disclaimer held.","OPTIONAL hands-only: the sticky note on a laptop bezel, and the printed one-pager landing on a desk."],"why_this_slot":"The story is 'one planner on Monday', so it goes out Sunday 7pm, when owners and small developers plan their week. Sunday evening is strong for YouTube and Facebook long-watch. It becomes the evergreen link that every site-research short points to (searches like 'what can I build on my section Auckland'). It's scoped so it doesn't repeat Tuesday's or Thursday's post: the requirements checklist and the calculator get a pointer only. It also takes in nz-strong-then-i-scrolled as its cold open."}]},{"week":3,"starts":"2026-10-19","theme":"Price it, compare it, sign it (estimate, tender, compare, agree)","posts":[{"date":"2026-10-20","time_nzt":"19:30","week":3,"idea_id":"qsme-nz-apples-oranges-pineapple","title":"Which one's cheapest?","existing_project":"","format":"short","kind":"skit (relatable)","length_s":33,"aspect":"9:16 (+4:5 Facebook feed cut)","platforms":["Instagram Reels","TikTok","Facebook Reels","Facebook feed (4:5)","YouTube Shorts"],"hook_text":"3 quotes. Same bathroom. Which one's cheapest?","concept":"Sunday night at a Hamilton kitchen bench: three bathroom quotes, '$8,298 incl. GST', '$7,400 + GST', and a flat '$7,900' from a sole trader who isn't GST-registered. 'So which one's cheapest?' '…Trick question.' 0-3s hook: three fictional quote props built in HTML (a generic PDF page, a text bubble '7400 + gst mate', a handwritten Kalam note), with a red marker circling $7,400 and a question mark. 3-7s: the shell-game gag, where the 'cheapest' ball lands under a different cup every shuffle. Caption: 'Depends how you read them.' 7-12s: 'Add a bid you received': the emailed PDF beside its draft, the tick 'I have checked this against the original and it is right.', then 'Received by you'. 12-18s: the 'Compare bids' table, Excl. GST | GST | Incl. GST. Caption: 'Bold = what they wrote. calc. = worked out at 15%.' 18-24s: on Excl. GST, 'Lowest' sits on the $8,298-incl. bidder; flip 'Compare on' to Incl. GST and 'Lowest' jumps to the $7,900 sole trader. 24-29s: punch in on '$7,400 + GST = $8,510. Never the cheapest.', then a row opens its 'Detailed price breakdown'. 29-33s end card: 'QSME doesn't pick the winner. It stops GST picking it for you. Free to join. qsme.co.nz'. Every bid is labelled 'Demo bids', with fictional businesses checked against the Companies Register. Never say 'line by line' or 'apples to apples'. It's a totals table, and each bid opens to its own lines. Ships silent with a two-voice Kiwi VO script for later. This reuses the existing inbox card qsme-nz-apples-oranges-pineapple (same 'Which one's cheapest?' line) instead of a new card: its old 'line-by-line comparison, same scope' payoff and the AI-generated character with fruit props are retired, replaced by this GST-basis twist and HTML props with no people. It also merges the skit and situation-demo versions of nz-which-ones-cheapest.","features":["Compare bids: totals excluding and including GST, with a 'Lowest' tag","Add a bid you received: emailed quotes join the comparison"],"owner_recordings_needed":["SESSION B (finish by Fri 16 Oct): a demo tender with three demo bids at exactly $8,298 incl. GST / $7,400 + GST / $7,900 not GST-registered, from fictional bidders.","Shot 1: 'Add a bid you received' with a fictional PDF quote: drop, 'Reading the quote…', the draft beside the original, the checked tick, 'Received by you'.","Shot 2: the 'Compare bids' table with a bold figure beside a grey 'calc.'.","Shot 3: flipping 'Compare on' from Excl. to Incl. GST with the 'Lowest' tag moving.","Shot 4: clicking a row to open its 'Detailed price breakdown'."],"why_this_slot":"This opens the pricing week with the most recognisable homeowner pain: spring reno quotes landing in different GST formats. Tuesday 7:30pm is the homeowner prime slot, and a 4:5 cut goes to the Facebook feed for older renovators. Compare-bids and add-a-bid don't appear in Saturday's long-form, which stops at what the bidder sees and points back here."},{"date":"2026-10-22","time_nzt":"06:15","week":3,"idea_id":"nz-thumbs-up-contract","title":"Our whole contract is a thumbs-up","existing_project":"","format":"short","kind":"skit (funny)","length_s":28,"aspect":"9:16","platforms":["Instagram Reels","TikTok","Facebook Reels","YouTube Shorts"],"hook_text":"Our entire contract: [thumbs-up]","concept":"A Tauranga owner agreed an $18,400 rewire with a sparky entirely by text. The thread ends 'Sweet as, see you Monday' and a thumbs-up, and three weeks in nobody knows whether the heat-pump wiring was included. 0-2.5s hook: a generic text thread (no iMessage or WhatsApp styling, fictional names) zooms until the emoji fills the frame. 2.5-10s: deadpan legal captions: 'Clause 1: [thumbs-up]', 'Heat pump wiring included? [thumbs-up] (unclear)', 'Payment stages? [thumbs-up] (also unclear)', then 'Agreed in every sense. Except a signature.' 10-16s: in Agreements, paste the thread, choose 'Write it up as a proper agreement', and the names and $18,400 come back, while anything the thread never stated (the payment stages, the finish date) stays a [blank]. Zoom on 'AI never makes up an amount, a date or a name. Anything it was not given stays a [blank] for you.' Then the owner types the scope line herself: 'Heat-pump wiring: included.' Caption: 'The thumbs-up didn't say. So you do.' VO: 'It won't make up the money or the dates. The heat pump's on me.' Film the real output: if the write-up fills or leaves anything differently, caption what it actually did and never re-stage it; the video makes no promise about scope items beyond the product's own line. 16-22s: the sparky's side, in the phone's browser as a test signer: 'I have read this agreement and I agree to sign it electronically.', a typed name, a finger signature, then 'Signed. Download a copy below and keep it.' 22-25s: the sender's list shows 'Everyone has signed', with a quick flash of the certificate page (test signer's email, network address and device blurred). 25-28s end card: 'Signed from a link. No account. No printer. Agreements is free on every plan. qsme.co.nz'. Small: 'Templates and write-ups are a starting point, not legal advice. The AI write-up uses a credit.' The joke is the emoji, never the sparky or the owner. Ships silent with a Mel VO script for later.","features":["Bring in an agreement you already have, or have QSME write notes up as one","Agreements signed from a link — no account needed"],"owner_recordings_needed":["SESSION B (finish by Fri 16 Oct): Agreements on desktop. Paste the fictional thread, then 'Write it up as a proper agreement', then the draft with the names and $18,400 and [blank]s where the thread gave no figure or date; zoom on the 'AI never makes up…' line; type the heat-pump scope line by hand; send to a test signer.","Phone browser screen recording as the test signer (not the iOS app): open the link, tick 'I have read this agreement…', type a fictional name, draw a finger signature, land on 'Signed. Download a copy below and keep it.'","Desktop: the sender's list moving to 'Everyone has signed', then the certificate page with the test signer's email, network address and device blurred.","OPTIONAL owner-filmed, hands only: a phone on a ute dashboard with a finger signing."],"why_this_slot":"This is week 3's funny short. Thursday 6:15am catches sparkies and tradies scrolling in the ute before 7, and the payoff is the sparky signing on his phone. Re-share it to Stories and Facebook at 7:30pm for owners. It lands just before Labour Day weekend and the pre-Christmas rush of small jobs agreed by text. The signing features don't appear in the week's other two posts."},{"date":"2026-10-24","time_nzt":"09:00","week":3,"idea_id":"nz-pricing-a-build-line-by-line","title":"Price it once: from the consent pack to one scope every trade prices","existing_project":"","format":"long","kind":"long-form walkthrough","length_s":140,"aspect":"16:9 (+9:16 chapter cut-downs)","platforms":["YouTube","Facebook","YouTube Shorts (chapter cuts)"],"hook_text":"Found before the tender. Not on site.","concept":"A Christchurch owner-builder has a consent pack thick enough to prop a door open and her brother-in-law's two-year-old spreadsheet. She needs a budget she trusts, and trades pricing the same scope, before the drainlayer prices a pack with no drainage sheet in it. 0:00-0:10 cold open: a printed demo pack thuds onto a bench (hands only) and the in-app notice 'Heads up: no drainage found in this pack.' slides in (a desktop notification, not an iPhone push; use whatever the real notice lists). 0:10-0:30: 'Read a document': 'Finding the sections…', section chips, and a window schedule opening as a real table. Tag: 'Paid plans; uses AI credits.' 0:30-0:50: in the SOQ, add a pocket, type a quantity and rate, and the line, job, pocket and Grand total tick up together. Add a Waste % column and a formula, then import the old spreadsheet as drafts. 0:50-1:10: 'Draft SOQ with AI' with 'AI draft' pockets (keep one, discard one), then 'Estimate with AI' fills only the unpriced lines, each marked, and editing one rate removes its mark. Caption: 'AI drafts. You check.' 1:10-1:25: 'Chat with AI' with the Beta tag and 'It can get things wrong' visible, and an answer labelled 'From your documents (… p.12)'; if the real answer carries no source label, cut the beat and never mock a citation. 1:25-1:50: the designer's drainage sheet arrives as its own PDF and is read in, then 'Create New Tender' from the Plumbing & drainage pocket: 'Select whole pocket', 'Point them at the right sheets' ticking the new drainage pages, a payment split with retention, invites to demo contacts. Never voice 'jump straight to those pages' (references are page rows, not a drawing viewer). 1:50-2:10: split screen of the owner's SOQ with rates beside the bidder's view of the same lines with the rate column empty. Caption: 'Same lines for every bidder. Your rates never shown.' Small: 'They can itemise or quote one total.' Pointer: 'Then compare the totals on the same GST basis: that's Tuesday's video.' 2:10-2:20 CTA: 'Price it once. Tender the same lines. Free to join. qsme.co.nz'. The title is changed so it never reads as a bid matrix or as every bid being itemised. The AI reads what drawings say and doesn't measure; mark-up is off and never shown; QSME has no rate library (AI rates are indicative and flagged for review). Captions are burned in, with a QS VO script for later.","features":["Read my plans / Plan index: consent pack split into sections","Schedule of Quantities (SOQ): pockets, jobs, lines, your own columns and formulas","SOQ in and out: Excel/CSV import, Excel/CSV/PDF export, templates","Draft SOQ with AI","Estimate with AI (prices only the unpriced lines, flagged for review)","Ask the plans: AI chat that labels where every number came from (Beta)","Tender cut from an SOQ pocket: private or public, invitations and share link","Tenders point each trade at the right sheets","What a bidder sees and how they price (rates and budget never shown)"],"owner_recordings_needed":["SESSION B (finish by Fri 16 Oct): a demo project with a sample plan set the owner has permission to show (no architect title block legible), with its drainage sheets saved as a separate PDF so the first read genuinely finds none. Label it 'Demo pack' on screen.","Shot A: 'Read a document' on the main pack, 'Finding the sections…', section chips, then the real 'Heads up: … no drainage found in this pack.' notice. Shot A2: reading the separate drainage PDF. Shot B: a chip opening to its table.","Shot C: typing in the SOQ, then adding a Waste % column and a formula. Shot D: importing assets/qsme/samples/qsme-example-soq.xlsx as drafts.","Shot E: 'Draft SOQ with AI', keeping one pocket and discarding another. Shot F: 'Estimate with AI', then editing one marked line.","Shot G: 'Chat with AI' with the Beta line visible and a cited 'From your documents' answer (only if the real answer carries the label).","Shot H: the 'Create New Tender' wizard from the Plumbing & drainage pocket: 'Select whole pocket', 'Point them at the right sheets' ticking the drainage pages, payment split and retention, invite demo contacts (private tender; never the public board).","Shot I: the bidder view on a second demo tradie account (not a main contractor account, which needs GC Pro to view opportunities): same lines with the rate column empty.","Filmed, hands only: a printed demo pack dropped on a bench."],"why_this_slot":"Saturday 9am on Labour Day weekend is when owner-builders plan summer builds and have time for a 2-minute watch. It also catches YouTube searches like 'how to price a house build NZ'. It's scoped to stop at what the bidder sees, so it doesn't repeat Tuesday's comparison or Thursday's agreement."}]},{"week":4,"starts":"2026-10-26","theme":"Build it: from Labour Day to the CCC (programme, site, handover)","posts":[{"date":"2026-10-26","time_nzt":"19:00","week":4,"idea_id":"nz-programme-survives-christmas","title":"How a programme survives Christmas","existing_project":"","format":"long","kind":"long-form walkthrough (comic cold open)","length_s":120,"aspect":"16:9 (+30s 9:16 'The January lie' cut)","platforms":["YouTube","Facebook","Instagram Reels (9:16 cut)","TikTok (9:16 cut)"],"hook_text":"Only one on your job working Christmas Day? Your spreadsheet.","concept":"An Auckland small developer doing four townhouses has a spreadsheet programme promising the buyer a mid-January handover. The cold open (0:00-0:12) takes over from nz-only-one-working-christmas: a generic spreadsheet (no brand UI) is cast as the keenest worker on the job. The '25 Dec · Framing' cell gets a marker Santa hat, and cells tick 'worked': Labour Day (today), the Boxing Day holiday (Mon 28 Dec this year), 1 Jan. Caption: 'Didn't even go to the bach. Handover 15 Jan. She'll be right.' 0:12-0:30: the same job in QSME from the 'Townhouses' template, with Days off set to 'This site works straight through Christmas and the New Year.' 0:30-0:50: switch to 'Christmas shutdown — Shut 23 December to 11 January, every year.', and the amber 'N jobs are planned on days the site is shut' banner appears (show the real count the demo returns). Press 'Move them off the days off' and the finish moves out. Caption: 'Only 4 of those days are public holidays. The rest still count as working days until you shut the site.' 0:50-1:05: a task dropped on Labour Day lands on the Tuesday with a toast; 'Works public holidays' goes on for the slab pour only; Auckland Anniversary is added by hand ('national holidays are built in; regional ones aren't'). 1:05-1:18: booked tradies are asked ('New dates — can you make them?'), and one confirms so the bar turns blue. Caption: 'In-app and email.' 1:18-1:35: 'What drives the finish date' and the purple chain. Dragging a grey task changes nothing; dragging a purple one moves the finish. 1:35-1:52: the A4 'Construction programme' PDF on demo letterhead, 'Any one of them running late moves the whole finish.', slid across a desk. 1:52-2:00 CTA: 'A finish date you can still say out loud in January. qsme.co.nz'. It celebrates a later date as the most useful thing the software does all year. Old programmes only move when the button is pressed. No 'bank-ready', no bank names, no durations quoted as promises, no SMS, no 'critical path'. Don't say the schedule is free. Captions are burned in, with a Kiwi VO script for later.","features":["Christmas shutdown and your own days off","NZ public holidays built in","Built-in NZ programmes (Townhouses, Standard NZ build, Land to titles)","Asked, not told: booked tradies confirm new dates","Linked tasks: one trade slips, everything after it moves","What drives the finish date (the purple chain)","Printed programme PDF (“Construction programme”)"],"owner_recordings_needed":["SESSION C (finish by Thu 22 Oct): desktop on a demo project with a demo letterhead set up, and the schedule visibility set to Trades (nothing is sent to tradies while it is Private).","Shot A: Templates, 'Townhouses', pick a start date, put it on. Shot B: Days off set to 'This site works straight through Christmas and the New Year.', with the FINISHES box visible.","Shot C: switching to 'The usual 23 Dec – 11 Jan', the amber 'N jobs are planned on days the site is shut' banner (real count), 'Move them off the days off', and the FINISHES box before and after.","Shot D: dropping a task on Labour Day and the holiday toast. Shot E: 'Works public holidays' switched on for the pour. Shot F: 'Add a day off' for Auckland Anniversary and a 'Crew away' week.","Shot G: 'Waiting for OK' chips, and a second demo tradie account confirming (mobile web or the email, not native iPhone schedule screens).","Shot H: the 'What drives the finish date' toggle, dragging a grey task, then a purple one.","Shot I: exporting 'As a PDF — the chart and the task list', plus the printed A4 slid across a desk (hands only)."],"why_this_slot":"It goes out on Labour Day itself (Mon 26 Oct, a public holiday), so the cold open's 'worked Labour Day' line lands the day it happens. 7pm catches people home from the long weekend and starting to plan the run to Christmas. October is when programmes that cross Christmas are set and buyers are given dates. It leads the build week with the schedule; this week's shorts cover the gate and the handover."},{"date":"2026-10-28","time_nzt":"06:00","week":4,"idea_id":"nz-pen-on-a-string","title":"The pen on a string","existing_project":"","format":"short","kind":"situation-demo (funny)","length_s":26,"aspect":"9:16","platforms":["Instagram Reels","TikTok","Facebook Reels","YouTube Shorts"],"hook_text":"MISSING: site pen. Last seen Tuesday.","concept":"A two-townhouse job in Christchurch. The sign-in book lives in a plastic sleeve on the gate, it rained Tuesday, the pen on the string has gone missing again, and Thursday's page is one smudge, right when someone asks who was on site Thursday. Framed as an object documentary. 0-2.5s: owner-filmed frayed string swinging empty on a gate in the rain. Text: 'MISSING: site pen. Last seen Tuesday.' 2.5-6s: a soggy prop book with invented scribbles. Caption: 'Who was on site Thursday? Book says: “D—”.' 6-10s: hands cut down a lost-cat-style MISSING poster and cable-tie a laminated QSME QR poster in its place. Caption, in the product's own words: 'Print it, laminate it, cable-tie it to the fence.' 10-16s: a gloved thumb scans; 'Site sign-in' opens with the demo builder's logo, then 'Who are you with?', the PPE rows ticked, 'Sign in for today' and 'You're signed in, [demo name]'. 16-19s: next morning, 'Welcome back' and one tap. Caption: 'No app. No account. No pen.' 19-23s, builder's side: 'Today on this site' grouped by company, 'Due today, not signed in yet', and the register PDF with invented names. 23-26s end card: 'One poster on the gate. qsme.co.nz'. Kiwi-bloke VO script for later: 'Q-S-me. Pen's still missing, mind.' It's a record, not a lock: no WorkSafe or HSWA claims, no GPS or sign-out times. The QR opens a web page, not the iPhone app.","features":["QR site sign-in poster and the on-site register"],"owner_recordings_needed":["SESSION C (finish by Thu 22 Oct), owner films hands only on their own fence or gate, with no identifiable site, signage or address: the frayed string swinging empty (rain if possible), a soggy prop sign-in book with invented scribbles, cutting down the MISSING poster, cable-tying a real laminated poster printed from the demo project, and a gloved thumb scanning.","Phone screen recording of the public sign-in page with an invented name: 'Site sign-in', 'Who are you with?', PPE ticks, 'Sign in for today', 'You're signed in'; then the next-day 'Welcome back'.","Desktop: the builder's 'Today on this site' page, 'Due today, not signed in yet', and the register PDF on demo letterhead with invented names."],"why_this_slot":"This is week 4's funny short, aimed at builders and site managers. Wednesday 6:00am catches them scrolling before the gate opens, midweek, so the idea is on site the same day. Spring rain is turning paper sign-in books to mush right now. Gate sign-in appears nowhere else this week."},{"date":"2026-11-01","time_nzt":"19:30","week":4,"idea_id":"qsme-nz-ccc-hunt","title":"Everything's finished. Except one bit of paper.","existing_project":"","format":"short","kind":"kinetic (relatable)","length_s":29,"aspect":"9:16","platforms":["Instagram Reels","TikTok","Facebook Reels","YouTube Shorts"],"hook_text":"Everything's finished. Except one bit of paper.","concept":"A Hamilton owner-builder has finished the house: painted, carpets down, and the family desperate to move in before Christmas. The CCC application is stuck on one missing Record of Work from the roofer, who's now on a job in Queenstown and answers texts on Sundays. 0-3s: stage-5-dusk b-roll (no people; labelled Illustrative), then a kinetic checklist that's all ticked except one red line, 'Record of Work — Roofing'. 3-8s, the hunt escalates: an inbox search for 'record of work' shows 'No results', then a garage box, then the ute glovebox, which turns up a mince-and-cheese pie receipt. Caption: 'Not it.' 8-13s: a text thread to 'Roofer' (fictional): Mon 'Need your Record of Work for the CCC, cheers', Wed 'Just bumping this', Fri 'Any chance?'. Caption: 'He's on a job in Queenstown. Fair enough.' 13-17s: caption 'Next build: the paper goes in while they're still on site.' 17-22s: a demo tradie records a certificate with 'Issue Certificate' and a dummy PDF, and the matching step on the 'Compliance & process checklist' ticks over. 22-26s: 'CCC Pack — Download every certificate plus the council index, as one zip', and the zip lands. Caption: 'QSME doesn't issue the CCC or lodge it. It just ends the hunt.' 26-29s end card: 'Start your project free at qsme.co.nz.' Small: 'Checklist templates and the CCC pack come with a paid plan.' The paperwork is the joke, never the roofer. Every certificate on screen is a dummy. Ships silent with a Mel VO script for later. This reuses the existing inbox card qsme-nz-ccc-hunt (same escalating hunt and glovebox-receipt gag) instead of a new card, moved from 'three years later, the lawyer asks' to the CCC application now, so the product is the fix for the next build rather than a promise about old paperwork.","features":["Trade certificates and the CCC pack (one zip for council)","Compliance & process checklist (templates by job type)"],"owner_recordings_needed":["SESSION C (finish by Thu 22 Oct): a demo project on a paid plan (or trial) with a demo tradie account. Shot 1: 'Issue Certificate' with a dummy PDF. Shot 2: the matching 'Compliance & process checklist' step ticking. Shot 3: the 'CCC Pack' zip downloading, then opened to show dummy files and the index. No real LBP numbers or names.","OPTIONAL owner-filmed, hands only: a ute glovebox opening onto a pie receipt."],"why_this_slot":"Sunday 7:30pm is owner-builders' planning time, and it pays off the 'answers texts on Sundays' gag. It runs into the pre-Christmas move-in rush, when everyone wants the CCC before the shutdown. It closes the four-week journey at handover, and the certificates and CCC pack appear nowhere else this week."}]}]},"global":{"weeks":[{"week":2,"starts":"2026-10-12","theme":"From six bids to one signed subcontract (Compare → Award → Sign). Every screen comes from owner recording Session A in one demo Global org.","posts":[{"date":"2026-10-13","time_nzt":"10:30 NZDT (Tue 08:30 Sydney/Melbourne AEDT, 07:30 Brisbane; Mon 22:30 London)","week":2,"idea_id":"qsme-build-trailer-parody","title":"The Transposition (a trailer)","existing_project":"","format":"short","kind":"skit","length_s":30,"aspect":"4:5","platforms":["LinkedIn (founder profile; page reposts at 07:30 London)","YouTube Shorts (9:16 re-export)"],"hook_text":"IN A WORLD… WHERE THE BID SAID 71,300…","concept":"Month seven on the job. Bid F, the whiteboard from Six bids, said 71,300. The subcontract says 73,100. It was typed from the recommendation email, which was typed from the comparison spreadsheet, which was typed from a photo of a whiteboard. It plays as a full blockbuster trailer in letterbox bars inside the 4:5 frame: 'IN A WORLD… WHERE THE BID SAID 71,300…' / '…AND THE CONTRACT SAID 73,100.', with the 1 and the 3 physically swapping places. Then 'ONE TRANSPOSITION.' / 'SEVEN MONTHS OF CORRESPONDENCE.', with quick cuts of 'RE: RE: RE: FW: Contract sum query', a 'WITHOUT PREJUDICE' header and a red-pen circle over stage-3-frame / th-4-structure b-roll, and 'THIS SEASON… THE CONTRACT IS MADE FROM THE BID.' Product (real, demo org): the 'Award this bid?' dialog naming Bidder F and 71,300 ('…creates the payment contract, and notifies all bidders'), the green Award button, the Active Work card (Contract value 71,300 · Retention · 'Milestones 0 / 3 confirmed'), and a 1s flash of the same programme task carrying the figure. Caption: 'Award becomes a contract, not a to-do.' Rating-card gag: 'RATED T — for Tender. Contains scenes of mild reconciliation.' on a generic card of our own, not a copy of any real ratings board's card. End card: 'Now showing at qsme.build.' Book a 30-minute demo. The cards carry the joke with sound off, and the trailer-read Kiwi VO script waits for ElevenLabs. This is the inbox's 'Trailer parody' card (same truth: the contract doesn't match the winning bid), sharpened. Guardrails: award is not signing. Never promise errors are impossible: the claim is only that the contract isn't retyped (an emailed or photographed bid is still read and checked by a person once). Crop currency and GST labels, and never caption the AI b-roll as a real project.","features":["Award in one click: the winning bid becomes a contract with milestones and retention","Tenders and contracts land on the schedule as the same task"],"owner_recordings_needed":["Session A (demo Global org set in Australia, invented companies, Six bids figures entered as bids A–F): Compare bids → Award on Bidder F → the 'Award this bid?' dialog showing Bidder F and 71,300 → the green Award button. Record this LAST in Session A, after every pre-award shot for 'Why six bids never compare', because award closes the tender.","Session A: Active Work card showing Contract value 71,300 · Retention · 'Milestones 0 / 3 confirmed' (currency and GST labels cropped)","Session A: the Plasterboard & ceilings task on the schedule after award, carrying the figure without '~' (put the tender on the programme before awarding)"],"why_this_slot":"Opens award week with the most shareable format, a trailer parody, in the Tuesday AU/NZ seeding slot. It picks up exactly where Six bids ended (Bid F won), and none of the week's other posts uses its feature (award → contract → the same programme task)."},{"date":"2026-10-14","time_nzt":"19:30 NZDT (Wed 07:30 London BST, 10:30 Dubai, 09:30 Riyadh)","week":2,"idea_id":"gl-why-bids-never-compare","title":"Why six bids never compare (it's the tender, not the subcontractors)","existing_project":"","format":"long","kind":"long-form","length_s":125,"aspect":"16:9","platforms":["LinkedIn (native video, founder profile; page reposts Thu 08:30 Sydney)","YouTube (chapters from the chapter cards)"],"hook_text":"6 bids. 0 that compare. It's not the subcontractors. It's the tender.","concept":"The Six bids props (the sideways PDF, the merged-cells laptop, the whiteboard photo) get pushed into a pile, and a hand lays one sheet titled 'Tender' on top. It's the same plasterboard & ceilings package: six bids, none comparable, and not the subcontractors' fault. There are five numbered causes, each with the mechanism that fixes it. (1) 'Everyone read the scope differently': the tender is cut from the SOQ pocket ('Select whole pocket'), and the bidder sees the same lines with the rate column empty ('Same lines for every bidder.'). (2) 'Two drawing revisions in circulation': 'Point them at the right sheets' and the bidder's 'Referenced drawing pages', and after v2 is uploaded, the amber 'pages may have moved'. (3) 'The scope changed on Tuesday and one bidder never heard': '2 bid(s) are in on this scope. Change it and everyone still in the running is told what moved.' (4) 'Plus tax, incl. tax': a kinetic example labelled 'example figures, VAT 20%' (10,000 + VAT vs 11,500 incl. VAT: the cheaper-looking bid isn't cheaper on the same basis). Then the real 'Compare on' flip with the 'Lowest' tag moving, but only if the recording check passes; otherwise chapter 4 stays kinetic. (5) 'It came by email': 'Add a bid you received' reads the PDF into a draft beside the original, with 'This was read automatically and can be wrong.' kept on screen. A person ticks the check, and it lands as 'Received by you'. The honest bit: click a row to open 'Detailed price breakdown' ('Totals side by side. Each bid's own lines one click away. You decide.'), with the Kiwi VO line 'It won't pick the winner for you. It's just a fair fight now.' The CTA uses the demo page's own offer ('Bring a real package. We will cut the tender, invite two test bidders and walk through the comparison and award on the call.') plus the comment prompt 'Which of the five gets you most?' It's silent-first with burned-in captions, and the Kiwi VO script is ideally voiced later by the founder. Guardrails: never 'compared line by line', no bidder-by-line matrix, never 'conforming', and QSME never picks the winner. Drawing references are plain rows (no viewer, no mark-up), and page references need the set read by 'Read my plans' (paid plan, AI credits). Say 'drawing set' and keep any 'consent pack' UI wording (e.g. 'Upload the consent pack…') out of frame. Never say GST: crop the tax headers, the toggle label and the 'calc.' note.","features":["Tender cut from an SOQ pocket: private or public, invitations and share link","Tenders point each trade at the right sheets","Read my plans / Plan index: consent pack split into sections","What a bidder sees and how they price (rates and budget never shown)","Compare bids: totals excluding and including GST, with a 'Lowest' tag","Add a bid you received: emailed quotes join the comparison"],"owner_recordings_needed":["Session A, recorded first (all shots below happen before the Transposition award, which closes the tender): 'Create New Tender' → 'Select whole pocket' on the Plasterboard & ceilings pocket → 'Copy link' (skip or crop 'Do your prices include GST?')","Session A: a second demo bidder account's view of the same lines ('Est Qty / Bid Qty / Rate') with the rate column empty","Session A: 'Point them at the right sheets' ticking sections of a demo drawing set QSME owns (already read by 'Read my plans'; any 'consent pack' wording out of frame), then the bidder's 'Referenced drawing pages' row","Session A: uploading v2 of that drawing set, then the bidder's amber 'pages may have moved'","Session A: editing the open tender with 2 bids in, showing '2 bid(s) are in on this scope. Change it and everyone still in the running is told what moved.'","Session A: 'Add a bid you received' with an emailed demo PDF → 'Reading the quote…' → the draft beside the original with 'This was read automatically and can be wrong.' → ticking 'I have checked this against the original and it is right.' → the 'Received by you' row","Session A: Compare bids with one demo bid entered as not tax-registered, flipping 'Compare on' with the 'Lowest' tag moving. Frame the rows and the tag; keep the tax headers, the toggle label and the 'calc.' note out of frame. First check that the worked-out figures use the org's own 10% (the product documents 15%); if they show 15%, skip this shot and keep chapter 4 kinetic.","Session A: clicking a row to open 'Detailed price breakdown'","Optional filmed, owner hands only: the printed Six bids props pushed into a pile and a 'Tender' sheet laid on top (otherwise recreated as kinetic from the Six bids motion source)"],"why_this_slot":"This is the long-form payoff for Six bids, posted a week later while the people who commented are still warm: it answers 'so what do I do about it?' Wednesday 07:30 London / 10:30 Dubai is the week's prime B2B slot, and the YouTube copy becomes the standing link for demo follow-ups. Its screens come from the same recording session as this week's two shorts, and their features don't overlap."},{"date":"2026-10-15","time_nzt":"19:30 NZDT (Thu 07:30 London BST, 10:30 Dubai, 09:30 Riyadh)","week":2,"idea_id":"gl-day-23","title":"Day 23 of one signature","existing_project":"","format":"short","kind":"kinetic","length_s":25,"aspect":"4:5","platforms":["LinkedIn (founder profile + page repost)","YouTube Shorts (9:16 re-export)"],"hook_text":"Subcontract signature: Day 23.","concept":"The ceilings subcontractor has been on site since Day 2, but the subcontract went out 23 days ago. A big mono counter rolls 01 → 23 over a stack of stamped, entirely innocent replies: Day 1 'Sent.' · Day 4 'Will sign tonight' · Day 9 'Can you resend? Went to junk' · Day 15 'Printer's out of toner' · Day 22 'Signed and scanned!' On Day 23 the scan arrives sideways, and its page strip reads 1 2 3 4 5 6 8 9… Caption: 'Page 7: retention.' Then: 'They've been on site since Day 2.' Product (real, demo org): our own subcontract PDF goes in via 'Sign it as it is', then 'Send for signing'. On a phone browser in a dusty hand, the signer ticks 'I have read this agreement and I agree to sign it electronically.', types the name, draws, and sees 'Signed. Download a copy below and keep it.' The sender's list flips from 'Out for signing' to 'Everyone has signed', with a flash of the certificate page. Captions: 'One link. No account. Nothing to install.' / 'The signed copy is the PDF you sent. Page 7 included.' Close: 'We still can't make them open it. We did make it one link.' Book a 30-minute demo, qsme.build. It's silent-first, with a Kiwi VO script for later. Guardrails: use only your own agreement (the built-in templates are written for NZ law, so keep the template list and any NZ-law line off screen). Never 'legally binding everywhere' or 'court-proof'. There's no signing order, no witnesses, no initials and no per-page signing. Signing happens in the phone's browser, not the iOS app. Crop the signer's network address, device and email from the certificate. The excuses stay innocent; the subcontractor is never the butt.","features":["Agreements signed from a link — no account needed","Bring in an agreement you already have, or have QSME write notes up as one"],"owner_recordings_needed":["Session A: Agreements → our own demo subcontract PDF → 'Sign it as it is' → 'Send for signing' to an invented signer (template list and NZ-law wording out of frame)","Session A: the sender's list moving from 'Out for signing' to 'Everyone has signed'","Session A: the certificate page for the invented signer (network address, device, email and any NZ-law line cropped)","Filmed, owner hands only: a phone on a dusty site table opening the signing link in the phone's browser, ticking the consent box, typing the name, drawing the signature and showing 'Signed. Download a copy below and keep it.'"],"why_this_slot":"Closes the week's arc (compare → award → sign) on Thursday morning in the UK and the Gulf, the day signatures get chased before the weekend. It's recorded in the same session as the award shots, and link signing appears nowhere else this week."}]},{"week":3,"starts":"2026-10-19","theme":"The programme: what really moves the finish date, and who's allowed to move it (Schedule → Build on site). Screens come from owner recording Session B.","posts":[{"date":"2026-10-20","time_nzt":"10:30 NZDT (Tue 08:30 Sydney/Melbourne AEDT, 07:30 Brisbane; Mon 22:30 London)","week":3,"idea_id":"gl-chase-the-painter","title":"Action: chase painter (week 6)","existing_project":"","format":"short","kind":"situation-demo","length_s":30,"aspect":"4:5","platforms":["LinkedIn (founder profile; page reposts at 07:30 London)","YouTube Shorts (9:16 re-export)"],"hook_text":"Week 6 minutes. Action: chase painter. (Again.)","concept":"It's the week-6 progress meeting. For six weeks the minutes have carried the same action, 'Chase painter (1 week late)', and the finish date moved anyway because of something nobody minuted: a late switchboard holding up the electrical fit-off. Arc: a generic minutes document scrolls through weeks 1–6 with the same highlighted line, and a pen underlines it a sixth time → 'Painter: 1 week late. Finish date: didn't move.' → a generic finish date ticks out 9 days → 'Something else moved it. Nobody minuted that.' Product (real, demo org): tick 'What drives the finish date', and a chain of bars gets purple rings and thick arrows. Drag the painter's grey bar a week right, and the last bar doesn't move ('Grey has room to move.'). Drag the purple electrical fit-off one day, and the finish moves a day ('Purple slips a day, the finish slips a day.'). Then the printed A4 'Construction programme' slides across a real desk, and the camera zooms on 'Any one of them running late moves the whole finish.' Caption: 'QSME calls it \"What drives the finish date\". You can call it the critical path.' Self-own close, borrowed from gl-wrong-phone-call: 'We owe the painter a coffee.' Book a 30-minute demo, qsme.build. It's silent-first, with a Kiwi VO script for later. Guardrails: 'critical path' is never shown as the UI label, there are no float numbers and no percentages, and money columns with tax labels stay hidden. The chain is off by default and only works on linked tasks. Switch off the default Christmas shutdown, and keep the dragged tasks unbooked. The grey-drag beat only works if the demo data really gives Painting a week of room: if the toast shows the finish moving, drag it two days and change the caption to match. The painter is innocent: the joke is on the meeting.","features":["What drives the finish date (the purple chain)","Linked tasks: one trade slips, everything after it moves","Printed programme PDF (“Construction programme”)"],"owner_recordings_needed":["Session B (demo Global project in the Australia org, about 20 linked tasks including 'Painting' and 'Electrical fit-off', Christmas shutdown switched off, nobody booked on the dragged tasks, and Painting given at least 5 working days of room before anything linked after it): ticking 'What drives the finish date' to show the purple chain","Session B: dragging the grey Painting bar a week right, with the finish unchanged and the toast visible (check the toast says the finish didn't move)","Session B: dragging the purple Electrical fit-off one day, with the finish moving a day","Session B: exporting 'As a PDF — the chart and the task list' with a demo letterhead set up","Filmed, owner hands only: the printed A4 'Construction programme' slid across a real desk, zooming on 'Any one of them running late moves the whole finish.' (shoot a second take, with a finger tracing the purple rings, for week 4)"],"why_this_slot":"Opens programme week in the Tuesday AU/NZ slot, early in the week when progress meetings happen. It's the 30-second set-up for week 4's 'A programme a lender can read', which goes deeper on the same printout, the same way Six bids set up the week-2 long-form."},{"date":"2026-10-21","time_nzt":"19:30 NZDT (Wed 07:30 London BST, 10:30 Dubai, 09:30 Riyadh)","week":3,"idea_id":"qsme-build-claude-writes-my-tender","title":"What your AI assistant can do in QSME, and the four things it never will","existing_project":"","format":"long","kind":"long-form","length_s":120,"aspect":"16:9","platforms":["LinkedIn (native video, founder profile; page reposts Thu 08:30 Sydney)","YouTube (chapters from the chapter cards)"],"hook_text":"Asked my AI to push the programme a week. It can't. On purpose.","concept":"9:40pm on the couch: a PM wants her AI assistant to do the week's typing, but won't let it near the dates, the money or the subcontractors. Hook (a real take): she asks it to push the programme back a week, it replies that it can't change the schedule, and we hard-cut to the QSME Gantt, untouched. If there's no clean take, fall back to: 'Asked it to send the tender. It can't.' Chapters: WHAT IT CAN SEE: 'What's on my schedule this week, and who's turning up?' gets a real answer from the demo project ('It sees what your login sees. Nothing more.'). WHAT IT CAN TYPE: 'Draft a tender for the façade package, private, closing next Friday.' In QSME the draft waits unpublished, and a pasted defects list becomes a sheet ('It drafts. A person presses send.'). WHAT IT CAN NEVER DO: the product's list, typed verbatim as kinetic ('Move money or record a payment · Send or publish a tender, or accept a bid · Issue, sign or cancel a contract · Change the schedule or delete a project'). WHO DID WHAT: Settings → Connected apps → 'Recent activity — What your connected apps have done, newest first.' → Disconnect: 'It will lose access immediately.' THE SMALL PRINT, said out loud: 'If an assistant tells you it has sent, paid or awarded something, it has not.', 'Every draft is a suggestion for a person to check' (qsme.build's own line) and 'ChatGPT needs a paid plan with developer mode.' Close: 'The assistant drafts; you decide.' If the founder agrees, add gl-first-year-rules' kicker as a caption: 'Same rules I had in my first year as a QS.' CTA: book a 30-minute demo, qsme.build. It's silent-first, with burned-in captions, chapter cards and a Kiwi VO script for later. This is the inbox card 'I asked Claude to write my tender' rebuilt around the never-list. Its old CTA ('Set it up in two minutes — qsme.build') is dropped because qsme.build has no sign-up and setup lives on the .co.nz /connect page. Guardrails: real outputs only. Discard any take where the assistant claims it did something it can't, mentions money, or uses NZ terms (GST, NZD, tradie, consent): the connector assumes NZD and NZ wording. No vendor logo as the hero and no implied partnership. Never 'summarise the bids'. No money in any prompt or answer. Crop api.qsme.co.nz, any qsme.co.nz domain on the OAuth screen, tokens and the setup command. Type the never list rather than capturing the .co.nz /connect page.","features":["Bring your own AI assistant: Claude, ChatGPT, Claude Code, Codex, Cursor (MCP)","Tender cut from an SOQ pocket: private or public, invitations and share link","Sheets: your own spreadsheets inside the project, linked to jobs and payments","Gantt timeline and numbered list for one project"],"owner_recordings_needed":["Session B: the owner's own assistant connected to the demo Global org (OAuth, with no token, setup command or qsme.co.nz domain on screen) asked 'What's on my schedule this week, and who's turning up?', with the real answer recorded (discard takes with money or NZ terms)","Session B: the assistant asked to push the programme back a week, its real reply that it can't change the schedule, then the untouched Gantt in QSME","Session B: 'Draft a tender for the façade package, private, closing next Friday', then the draft waiting unpublished in QSME with the send step untouched","Session B: a pasted demo defects list → 'put it in QSME as a sheet' → the sheet in the project","Session B: Settings → Connected apps → the 'Recent activity' list → Disconnect → 'It will lose access immediately.' (api.qsme.co.nz and any personal chats cropped)"],"why_this_slot":"AI is the most argued-about topic on construction LinkedIn, so it gets the week's prime Wednesday UK/Gulf slot and a YouTube upload. In programme week it answers 'who's allowed to move the dates?': the assistant can read the programme but never change it. Its features don't overlap the painter short or the diary short."},{"date":"2026-10-22","time_nzt":"19:30 NZDT (Thu 07:30 London BST, 10:30 Dubai, 09:30 Riyadh)","week":3,"idea_id":"gl-as-above","title":"Wednesday: As above.","existing_project":"","format":"short","kind":"skit","length_s":26,"aspect":"4:5","platforms":["LinkedIn (founder profile + page repost)","YouTube Shorts (9:16 re-export)"],"hook_text":"Site diary. Written Friday, 4:52pm. For Monday.","concept":"Friday 4:52pm in the site office: the whole week's diary gets written from memory. In an overhead phone shot of a paper diary and a hi-vis sleeve, the entries shrink: MON gets a paragraph → TUE 'Works progressed.' → WED 'As above.' → THU 'As above.' → FRI 'Ditto.' It rained on Tuesday until 11. Card: 'Nine months later.' A generic email asks, 'Can you confirm whether Tue 13 Oct was a rain day?' The diary's Tuesday says 'Works progressed.' Caption: 'It was. Probably.' Product (real, demo org, on the web): log a 'Rain day' for Tue 13 Oct and press 'Push these tasks'. The 'What happened' panel shows 'Rain day — Tue 13 Oct', and the Daily log already has 'Rain day' and 'Schedule change' entries ('Log the rain day once. It writes itself into the diary.'). A chat photo is marked 'Mark as proof of work' and appears in that day's log. Then, on the phone's browser, 'Add a note for today…' and 'Add photo' ('Photos land on the day you post them. Not on Friday.'). Close: 'It doesn't write the whole diary. It writes the bits you'd forget by Friday.' Book a 30-minute demo, qsme.build. It's a first-person confession, so the joke is on us, not on site managers. It's silent-first, with a Kiwi VO script for later. Guardrails: only some entries write themselves, and a person logs the rain day (the forecast only shades the chart). It isn't a legal record or an extension-of-time tool, and photos aren't GPS- or time-verified. There's no video upload and no offline mode. Film the phone moment in the phone's browser at app.qsme.build, not the iPhone app: the app build defaults to the NZ market and isn't adapted for global accounts. Check that the Rain day dialog accepts a past date, and which day the automatic daily-log entries file under. If either is the recording day rather than Tue 13 Oct, use the recording date throughout and change the email's date to match.","features":["Daily log with photos (a site diary that partly writes itself)","Rain days and the rain outlook","Messages: project chat, direct and group, photos, files and voice notes","What happened: full history, plain-words reasons, and Undo"],"owner_recordings_needed":["Filmed, owner hands only: a real paper site diary on a site-office desk with the five shrinking entries written in, hi-vis sleeve, no faces (fallback: Kalam handwriting animation in the motion kit)","Session B (web, demo project with weather-sensitive tasks): the 'Rain day' dialog ticking Tue 13 Oct → 'What moves' → 'Push these tasks'","Session B: the 'What happened' entry 'Rain day — Tue 13 Oct'","Session B: the Daily log with the automatic 'Rain day' and 'Schedule change' entries (note which day they file under)","Session B: a chat photo → 'Mark as proof of work' → appearing in that day's log","Session B, phone browser at app.qsme.build (not the iPhone app, whose build defaults to the NZ market): 'Add a note for today…' and 'Add photo' on the daily log"],"why_this_slot":"Lands Thursday morning in the UK and the Gulf, the day before everyone writes their Friday diary, as northern-hemisphere rain-day season starts. It ties programme week to the site: a rain day logged on the programme writes itself into the diary. Tue 13 Oct is a real date nine days earlier, which makes the 'nine months later' question concrete."}]},{"week":4,"starts":"2026-10-26","theme":"Money people: what the funder, the accounts team and procurement ask for (Pay + the decision stage). Screens come from owner recording Session C; UK clocks go back on 25 Oct, so the UK/Gulf slot moves to 20:30 NZDT.","posts":[{"date":"2026-10-27","time_nzt":"10:30 NZDT (Tue 08:30 Sydney/Melbourne AEDT, 07:30 Brisbane; Mon 21:30 London GMT)","week":4,"idea_id":"gl-remittance-attached","title":"Remittance attached. (It wasn't.)","existing_project":"","format":"short","kind":"kinetic","length_s":27,"aspect":"4:5","platforms":["LinkedIn (founder profile; page reposts at 07:30 London)","YouTube Shorts (9:16 re-export)"],"hook_text":"'Remittance attached.' (It wasn't.)","concept":"Month-end. The subcontractor's accounts team writes 'Claim 6 not received.' The main contractor's accounts team replies 'Paid on the 14th. Remittance attached.' There is no attachment: the cursor scrolls to the bottom looking for a paperclip that isn't there, to one lonely notification ping. The thread escalates one reply per beat: 'Nothing attached?' · 'Reattached' · 'That's March's' · 'Which account?' · 'Can we jump on a call?' A split screen shows claim 6 as PAID in one spreadsheet and OUTSTANDING in the other: 'Both correct. According to themselves.' Product (real, demo contract, two accounts): the contractor's milestone 'Submit progress' with a site photo and their invoice. The payer clicks 'I paid outside QSME', enters a 'Payment reference', ticks 'I have sent the money.' and presses 'Mark as paid', which shows 'Awaiting confirmation' ('Only the payer can mark it paid.'). The receiver ticks 'The money has cleared in my account.', giving 'Settled outside QSME and confirmed by both sides.', and a flash of Project money shows 'Cost paid' updating ('Only the receiver can confirm it.'). Close: 'QSME never touches the money. It keeps the one record you both ticked.' Book a 30-minute demo, qsme.build. It's silent-first, with a Kiwi VO script for later. The two-voice hook from gl-i-paid-you-last-week ('I paid you Friday.' / 'Nothing's landed.') is kept as the paid-test alternative. Guardrails: never 'secure payments', 'release the funds', escrow or payment protection, and never imply QSME verifies the transfer. No bank button and no Dispute button on screen. Crop currency and GST labels, and never reuse the payments.mp4 or awarded-work.mp4 audio or visuals. The spreadsheets are the butt, not either accounts team.","features":["Milestones: proof of work, invoice, then 'paid outside QSME' confirmed by the receiver","Project money page: estimate vs actual, every stage, your own money in and out"],"owner_recordings_needed":["Session C (demo Global contract with three milestones and two demo accounts, payer and receiver): the receiver's Stage 2 'Submit progress' with a demo site photo and invoice PDF","Session C: the payer's 'I paid outside QSME' → a dummy 'Payment reference' → 'I have sent the money.' → 'Mark as paid' → 'Awaiting confirmation'","Session C: the receiver ticking 'The money has cleared in my account.' → 'Settled outside QSME and confirmed by both sides.'","Session C: the Project money page with 'Cost paid' updating (currency and GST labels cropped)"],"why_this_slot":"Posts in the last week of October, while month-end claims and payment runs are live (31 Oct is a Saturday, so runs land Thursday–Friday). It's the most relatable opener of money week, in the Tuesday AU/NZ slot, and the month's only payment story."},{"date":"2026-10-28","time_nzt":"20:30 NZDT (Wed 07:30 London GMT, 11:30 Dubai, 10:30 Riyadh)","week":4,"idea_id":"gl-a-programme-a-lender-can-read","title":"A programme a lender can read","existing_project":"","format":"long","kind":"long-form","length_s":122,"aspect":"16:9","platforms":["LinkedIn (native video, founder profile; page reposts Thu 08:30 Sydney)","YouTube (chapters from the chapter cards)"],"hook_text":"Your programme. As the lender sees it.","concept":"The developer's funder wants an updated programme before the next drawdown review. The PM sends a Gantt screenshot that runs off the page, has no dates on the bars, and repeats one 450,000 contract on six rows, so the cost line reads 2,700,000 (tagged 'example figures'). Hook: that screenshot zooms out to unlabelled sticks to a printer whirr. Six fixes then land on one A4. (1) From the estimate: 'Import jobs from SOQ', and the priced jobs land end to end, each taking its own Days, with no retyping. Links are added by hand; the import doesn't plan or link anything. (2) Slips and rain: drag a task and the toast says what moved; a booked subcontractor is asked, not told ('New dates — can you make them?'); a rain day's 'What moves' preview. (3) What drives the finish: the purple chain, a callback to last week's painter. (4) Your days off: 'Add a day off' for the site's own holidays → '12 jobs are planned on days the site is shut' → 'Move them off the days off'. (5) Money, once: the Cost and Paid columns, with '~' on estimates and each contract counted once. (6) Print: 'As a PDF — the chart and the task list' gives an A4 'Construction programme' on the developer's letterhead, with ON SITE FROM · FINISHES · HOW LONG · TASKS · NEXT MILESTONE and 'Any one of them running late moves the whole finish.' Hands slide it across a desk, and a finger traces the purple rings. CTA: 'Bring your programme spreadsheet to a 30-minute demo · qsme.build.' It's silent-first, with chapter cards and a Kiwi VO script for later. Guardrails: never 'bank-approved', 'bank-ready' or 'accepted by lenders', and never name a lender (the reader is fictional and hands-only). The claim is that a lender can read it, never that a lender accepts it or that it releases a drawdown. Holidays are 'add your own days off' only, since QSME has no local public-holiday calendar, and the default shutdown is switched off. 'Critical path' is never a UI word; no percentages, cash-flow or float. Asked-not-told runs in-app and by email only. Crop currency and GST labels on screen and in the PDF's money boxes. Say 'subcontractor' or 'crew', never 'tradie'.","features":["Fill the schedule from the estimate (SOQ import)","Linked tasks: one trade slips, everything after it moves","Asked, not told: booked tradies confirm new dates","Rain days and the rain outlook","What drives the finish date (the purple chain)","Christmas shutdown and your own days off","Money on the programme: Cost and Paid columns, estimate vs agreed","Printed programme PDF (“Construction programme”)","Everything you send goes out under your logo, not QSME's"],"owner_recordings_needed":["Session C (demo Global project in the Australia org, shutdown switched off, demo letterhead set up): 'Import jobs from SOQ' → 'Import 23 tasks' with the bars landing end to end, then linking them by hand (the purple chain needs linked tasks)","Session C: dragging a task, with the toast naming what moved and what was sent for OK (schedule set to Trades, not Private)","Session C: 'Send for their OK' plus the second demo account's 'New dates — can you make them?' card","Session C: the Rain day 'What moves' preview","Session B footage reused: the purple chain toggled on, a grey drag (no change) and a purple drag (finish moves)","Session C: 'Add a day off' → '12 jobs are planned on days the site is shut' → 'Move them off the days off'","Session C: the Cost and Paid columns with '~' estimates against agreed prices and the 'Contracted · Paid · Still to contract ~' line","Session C: the 'As a PDF — the chart and the task list' export of the A4 'Construction programme' on the demo letterhead (currency in the money boxes cropped, or money boxes switched off)","Filmed, owner hands only: the printed A4 slid across a real desk with a finger tracing the purple rings (second take from the week-3 shoot)"],"why_this_slot":"Takes the prime Wednesday UK/Gulf slot in money week, framed around a drawdown review (the funder's question). It's the deep-dive payoff for week 3's painter short, the same way 'Why six bids never compare' paid off Six bids, and the shoot also produces the first sample programme PDF for sales."},{"date":"2026-10-29","time_nzt":"20:30 NZDT (Thu 07:30 London GMT, 11:30 Dubai, 10:30 Riyadh)","week":4,"idea_id":"gl-iso-27001-no","title":"Is QSME ISO 27001 certified? No.","existing_project":"","format":"short","kind":"kinetic","length_s":30,"aspect":"4:5","platforms":["LinkedIn (founder profile + page repost)","YouTube Shorts (9:16 re-export)"],"hook_text":"Is QSME ISO 27001 certified?","concept":"A main contractor's procurement lead is on row one of a vendor security questionnaire, 'ISO 27001 certified? Y/N', after a month of vendors answering with badge walls and roadmaps. The arc is a live capture of qsme.build/security scripted with capture.mjs. The FAQ 'Is QSME ISO 27001 certified?' opens to 'No.' and holds for 1.5s. 'Do you have a SOC 2 report?' opens to 'No. We do not claim one.' ('Also no.'). Then a scroll to 'We do not display badges, logos or ratings we have not earned.', and the 'What is in place today?' section, led by 'We publish what is in place and nothing more.' The 'stripped out server-side' sentence was the week-1 Column H proof, so it gets only a 1s callback here. Then the programme status, verbatim: 'An information security management system aligned to ISO/IEC 27001:2022 is being built now.' ('In progress. Not certified. Said plainly.'). CTA: the page's own heading 'Need this in writing for procurement?' → Book a demo · qsme.build. Both headings were checked live on 2 Oct. It's deadpan, silent-first type using verbatim quotes only, with a Kiwi VO script for later. It needs the owner's sign-off on leading with 'No'. Guardrails: never paraphrase upward ('certified soon', 'by 2027', 'basically compliant'). No badges, and no SSO, MFA, GDPR, pen-test or data-residency claims (don't open those FAQs). Don't caption, zoom on or hold the 'Encrypted daily backups' card: nightly backups are only merged and can't be verified, so at most it scrolls past. Crop the footer's +64 phone number and .co.nz email.","features":["Honest security page (ISO 27001 in progress, not certified)","Book a demo and the five audience pages (global)"],"owner_recordings_needed":[],"why_this_slot":"Closes the month at the decision stage: after four weeks of how it works, it answers the first question procurement asks, in one word. Thursday 07:30 London / 11:30 Dubai. It needs no login, so if a Session C recording slips it can move earlier as a buffer without posting any mocked-up product screen."}]}]}},"ideas":[{"id":"nz-thumbs-up-contract","edition":"nz","title":"Our whole contract is a thumbs-up","kind":"skit","format":"short","length_s":28,"aspect":"9:16","platforms":["IG Reels","TikTok","Facebook Reels","YouTube Shorts"],"situation":"A Tauranga owner agreed $18,400 with a sparky for a rewire entirely over text. The thread ends 'Sweet as, see you Monday' and a thumbs-up emoji. Three weeks in, nobody can say whether the heat pump wiring was included.","truth":"Half the small jobs in NZ are agreed by text and sealed with a thumbs-up, because nobody wants to print, sign, scan and email.","twist":"The thumbs-up gets a deadpan legal reading ('Clause 1: thumbs-up'). Then QSME writes the thread up as a proper agreement and refuses to guess what the emoji meant: the heat pump line comes back as a [blank] for you to fill. The sparky then signs on his phone in the ute: no account, no printer.","hook":{"text":"Our entire contract:","verbal":"Here's our whole contract for the rewire.","visual":"A generic text thread scrolls to 'Sweet as, see you Monday' and zooms into a giant thumbs-up emoji that fills the frame.","audio":"A message 'sent' whoosh, then one dry gavel tap."},"beats":["0-2.5s HOOK: a generic text thread (fictional names) ending 'Sweet as, see you Monday' plus a thumbs-up, zooming until the emoji fills the frame. Text: 'Our entire contract:'. VO (Mel, later): 'Here's our whole contract for the rewire.'","2.5-7s: deadpan legal captions over the emoji: 'Clause 1: [thumbs-up]', 'Heat pump wiring included? [thumbs-up] (unclear)', 'Payment stages? [thumbs-up] (also unclear)'. VO: 'Clause one. Thumbs up. That's it. That's all the clauses.'","7-10s: caption: 'Agreed in every sense. Except a signature.'","10-16s: owner recording, Agreements: paste the thread, choose 'Write it up as a proper agreement', and the names and $18,400 come back while the heat pump wiring stays a [blank]. Zoom on: 'AI never makes up an amount, a date or a name. Anything it was not given stays a [blank] for you.' VO: 'It won't guess what the thumbs-up meant. You fill the blank.'","16-22s: owner recording on a phone browser, the sparky's side (test signer): open the link, 'I have read this agreement and I agree to sign it electronically.', type the name, draw a finger signature, then 'Signed. Download a copy below and keep it.'","22-25s: the sender's list flips to 'Everyone has signed', with a quick flash of the certificate page.","25-28s: end card: 'Signed from a link. No account. No printer.' / 'Agreements is free on every plan — qsme.co.nz'"],"features":[{"name":"Bring in an agreement you already have, or have QSME write notes up as one","status":"live"},{"name":"Agreements signed from a link — no account needed","status":"live"}],"journey_stage":"Agree & sign","job":"organic-then-paid","footage_plan":[{"kind":"skit-ui","what":"Motion kit: a generic messaging thread (not iMessage or WhatsApp branding; fictional names), the emoji zoom and the legal-caption overlays."},{"kind":"owner-recording","what":"OWNER, week 2+: Agreements, pasting the fictional thread, then 'Write it up as a proper agreement', the draft with a [blank], and the 'AI never makes up…' line."},{"kind":"owner-recording","what":"OWNER, week 2+: a phone screen recording in the phone's browser as a test signer, from the link through to 'Signed. Download a copy below and keep it.'; then the sender's 'Everyone has signed' and the certificate page on desktop."},{"kind":"filmed","what":"Optional, owner films, hands only: a phone on a ute dashboard with a finger signing."}],"cta":"Turn the thumbs-up into something signed: Agreements is free on every plan at qsme.co.nz.","scores":{"recognizable":5,"specific":5,"ownable":3,"shareable":5},"why_now":"Agreements (write, send, sign from a link) shipped 21-23 Sep and is free on every plan. The pre-Christmas rush of small jobs gets agreed by text.","risks":["Templates and AI write-ups are a starting point, not legal advice. Never 'legally binding everywhere' or 'lawyer-approved'.","The AI write-up uses one credit (free plans have limited AI uses). Agreements itself is free, but don't imply the AI is.","Signing happens in the phone's browser, not inside the iOS app.","Don't state the $30k written-contract rule unless it's verified. This job is under it anyway.","The joke is the emoji, never the sparky or the owner."]},{"id":"nz-pen-on-a-string","edition":"nz","title":"The pen on a string","kind":"situation-demo","format":"short","length_s":26,"aspect":"9:16","platforms":["IG Reels","TikTok","Facebook Reels","YouTube Shorts"],"situation":"A builder on a two-townhouse job in Christchurch. The site sign-in book lives in a plastic sleeve on the gate, it rained Tuesday, the pen on the string has gone missing again, and Thursday's page is one smudge. Then someone asks who was on site Thursday.","truth":"The paper sign-in book is wet, missing its pen, and useless the one time anyone actually needs it.","twist":"An object documentary: a lost-cat-style 'MISSING: site pen' poster on the fence gets cut down, and a laminated QR poster is cable-tied in the same spot, following the product's own instruction: 'Print it, laminate it, cable-tie it to the fence.' The callback: the pen is still missing, mind.","hook":{"text":"MISSING: site pen. Last seen Tuesday.","verbal":"Has anyone seen the pen? The one on the string?","visual":"Close-up of a frayed string cable-tied to a site gate, swinging empty in the rain.","audio":"Rain on corrugated iron."},"beats":["0-2.5s HOOK: owner-filmed frayed string on a gate, no pen, rain. Text: 'MISSING: site pen. Last seen Tuesday.' VO (Kiwi bloke, later): 'Has anyone seen the pen? The one on the string?'","2.5-6s: a soggy prop sign-in book with invented scribbles; Thursday's page is one smudge. Caption: 'Who was on site Thursday? Book says: \"D—\".'","6-10s: hands cut down the MISSING poster and cable-tie a laminated QSME QR poster in its place. Caption: 'Print it, laminate it, cable-tie it to the fence.'","10-16s: a gloved thumb and phone camera scan the code. The 'Site sign-in' page opens with the demo builder's logo, then 'Who are you with?', PPE rows ticked, 'Sign in for today' and 'You're signed in, [demo name]'.","16-19s: next morning, 'Welcome back, [demo name]', one tap. Caption: 'No app. No account. No pen.'","19-23s: builder's side (owner recording): 'Today on this site' grouped by company, 'Due today, not signed in yet', and the register PDF on letterhead (invented names).","23-26s: end card: 'One poster on the gate.' qsme.co.nz. VO: 'Q-S-me. Pen's still missing, mind.'"],"features":[{"name":"QR site sign-in poster and the on-site register","status":"live"}],"journey_stage":"Build on site","job":"organic","footage_plan":[{"kind":"filmed","what":"OWNER films, week 2+, hands only and no identifiable site or address: a frayed string on a gate, a soggy prop sign-in book with invented scribbles, cutting down the MISSING poster, cable-tying a real laminated QR poster printed from a demo project, a gloved thumb scanning."},{"kind":"owner-recording","what":"OWNER, week 2+: a phone screen recording of the public sign-in page with an invented name ('Site sign-in' to 'Who are you with?' to the PPE ticks to 'You're signed in'), then 'Welcome back'; the builder's 'Today on this site' page and the register PDF with invented names."},{"kind":"kinetic","what":"Motion kit: the MISSING poster artwork, captions, end card."}],"cta":"One poster on the gate, no app or account for whoever walks on: qsme.co.nz.","scores":{"recognizable":5,"specific":5,"ownable":3,"shareable":4},"why_now":"QR sign-in shipped on 22 Sep, and spring rain is turning paper sign-in books to mush.","risks":["No WorkSafe or Health and Safety at Work Act compliance claims. It's a record, not a lock.","No GPS, sign-out times or identity verification claims.","The QR code opens a web page, not the iOS app. Don't show the app.","Only invented names on any register. QR sign-in apps are common, so 'ownable' depends on the gag, not the feature.","The owner must film it (week 2+)."]},{"id":"nz-granny-flat-for-mum","edition":"nz","title":"Granny flat for Mum","kind":"situation-demo","format":"short","length_s":37,"aspect":"9:16","platforms":["Instagram Reels","TikTok","Facebook Reels","YouTube Shorts"],"situation":"A Kiwi family in Auckland want a 60 m² granny flat in the back yard so Mum can move closer. They've heard the new granny-flat rules mean 'no consent'. Their place is Flat 1 on a cross-lease. One of them opens Site research on the section the night before ringing a builder.","truth":"Everybody's heard granny flats got easier. Nobody's mentioned that on a cross-lease, the people next door still get a say.","twist":"The good news and the catch arrive in the same checklist. One row says 'Granny flat without consent… can often be added… where the site meets the conditions'. Then, in red, 'Other cross-lease owners' consent', because: Cross-lease title. The deciding vote belongs to Flat 2. Better bake something.","hook":{"text":"Granny flat for Mum. 'No consent needed now', right?","verbal":"We heard granny flats don't need consent any more. Then we checked the section.","visual":"Illustrative b-roll of an empty back yard turning into a small granny flat (gf clips, labelled 'Illustrative'), hard cut to a red REQUIRED badge stamping onto 'Other cross-lease owners' consent'.","audio":"A light, hopeful two-note pluck, cut off by a single rubber-stamp thud."},"beats":["0–3s HOOK: gf-1-idea into gf-3-placed b-roll (labelled 'Illustrative'), then cut to the red REQUIRED row. CAPTION: 'Granny flat for Mum. “No consent needed now”, right?' VO: 'We heard granny flats don't need consent any more.'","3–10s: the 'What could go here' zone card, with the green 'Without resource consent' list and the blue 'With a resource consent' list. CAPTION: 'The zone side looks good.' VO: 'Zone side? Looks good.'","10–17s: 'What this site would require', on the 'Granny flat without consent' row, with a highlighter over 'can often' and 'where the site meets the conditions'. CAPTION: '“Can often.” Read the conditions.' VO: 'Can often. Where the site meets the conditions. Righto.'","17–25s: tap the 'Required' filter and the list collapses to the red REQUIRED row 'Other cross-lease owners' consent — On a cross-lease the other flat owners must consent to any change of footprint…'. Zoom on 'because: Cross-lease title'. CAPTION: 'Because it's a cross-lease. Flat 2 gets a say.' VO: 'Then this. We're a cross-lease, so next door gets a say.'","25–31s: the 'LIM & Record of Title — Order a LIM and the title before committing…' row. CAPTION: 'Next: the LIM, the title, and a cuppa with next door.' VO: 'So: LIM, title, and a cuppa with next door. Might bake something.'","31–37s CTA: end card. CAPTION: 'See what your section would require. Free account. Auckland. qsme.co.nz' VO: 'Check yours first. Q-S-me dot co dot en zed.'"],"features":[{"name":"“What this site would require” checklist","status":"live"},{"name":"Zone-first zoning: “What could go here” and the coloured zoning map","status":"live"},{"name":"Site research page (web, free account)","status":"live"}],"journey_stage":"Find a site → Feasibility","job":"organic-then-paid","footage_plan":[{"kind":"owner-recording","what":"Weeks 2–3, signed in, on an Auckland cross-lease residential section (the owner's own, or a consenting friend's). Shot A: the 'What could go here' zone card. Shot B: 'What this site would require' showing the 'Granny flat without consent' row. Shot C: tapping the 'Required' filter, landing on 'Other cross-lease owners' consent' with 'because: Cross-lease title'. Shot D: the 'LIM & Record of Title' row. Record cards only: no address bar, map, parcel shape or legal description."},{"kind":"clip-broll","what":"gf-1-idea, gf-3-placed and gf-5-movein (AI brand b-roll from the homepage) as 1–2 s cutaways, labelled 'Illustrative'."},{"kind":"kinetic","what":"Highlighter on the conditional words, a stamp on REQUIRED, captions and the end card."}],"cta":"See what your Auckland section would require, with a free account at qsme.co.nz.","scores":{"recognizable":4,"specific":5,"ownable":4,"shareable":4},"why_now":"Granny flats are the hot housing topic under the new national rules, and families plan summer builds in spring. The requirements checklist with its 'because:' lines shipped on 30 September.","risks":["Keep the conditional wording on screen ('can often… where the site meets the conditions'). Never say 'build a granny flat with no consent'.","Both rows must genuinely appear for a real Auckland site. If no single site shows both, cut them as two sites and don't imply they're one.","Tenure is read from the legal-description wording, which is a clue, not a title search. Never say QSME checked the title.","Residential privacy: cards only. No address, map or legal description on screen.","Don't use the homepage scroll-story line 'Up to 70 m² with no building consent'.","The joke is the cross-lease situation, never the neighbours."]},{"id":"nz-walk-away-number","edition":"nz","title":"The walk-away number","kind":"situation-demo","format":"short","length_s":35,"aspect":"9:16","platforms":["Instagram Reels","TikTok","Facebook Reels","YouTube Shorts"],"situation":"Saturday, 9:40am, in a car park outside an Auckland auction. A first-time small developer is twenty minutes from bidding on a section she has already researched, and everyone inside is about to 'feel it' on the day. She opens the Rough feasibility calculator one last time.","truth":"At an auction you set your limit with your heart. Your margin finds out later.","twist":"The calculator works backwards. The green box 'What you could pay for the land to keep a 20% margin' is a walk-away number, worked out in the car before the room gets loud. Put in the builder's real rate and the margin goes red: the most useful buzzkill in property.","hook":{"text":"20 minutes before the auction. One number.","verbal":"Twenty minutes before the auction, I worked out my walk-away number.","visual":"Owner-filmed phone shot from the driver's seat: the car park through the windscreen, the dashboard clock at 9:40, a plain numbered card on the passenger seat. Cut to the green land-price box counting up, tagged 'Illustrative numbers'.","audio":"The car's indicator ticking, then it stops."},"beats":["0–3s HOOK (filmed). CAPTION: '20 minutes before the auction. One number.' VO: 'Twenty minutes out. Worked out my walk-away number.'","3–9s: the section's Site research, 'Development potential' with 'Homes with consent — indicative range', scrolling to 'Rough feasibility — Your numbers, our arithmetic.' and 'Homes — prefilled from the report'. CAPTION: 'Homes: from the site report. Indicative.' VO: 'The homes number comes from the site report.'","9–17s: type a Land price and a Sale price per home (tagged 'Illustrative numbers'). Profit and Margin on cost flip from '—' to figures. CAPTION: 'My numbers. Its arithmetic.' VO: 'I put in the land price and what they'd sell for.'","17–23s: raise 'Build cost per m² — edit to your builder's rate' until Margin on cost turns red under 20%. CAPTION: 'Builder's real rate in. Margin goes red.' VO: 'Then my builder's actual rate. Ouch.'","23–29s: the green box 'What you could pay for the land to keep a 20% margin:' held full-frame. CAPTION: 'That's the paddle limit.' VO: 'That's my limit. Over that, the paddle stays down.'","29–35s: small-print card 'Rough: no GST, tax, holding costs or agent fees. Not advice. Run it past your builder, QS and bank.', then the end card. CAPTION: 'Run your numbers before the room does. qsme.co.nz' VO: 'Run yours before the room does. Q-S-me dot co dot en zed.'"],"features":[{"name":"Rough feasibility calculator (“Your numbers, our arithmetic.”)","status":"live"},{"name":"Development potential verdict and indicative yield","status":"live"},{"name":"Site research page (web, free account)","status":"live"}],"journey_stage":"Feasibility","job":"organic-then-paid","footage_plan":[{"kind":"filmed","what":"Optional, owner films 3 s from the driver's seat in any car park: dashboard clock and a plain numbered card on the seat. No faces, number plates, auction-house signage or branding."},{"kind":"owner-recording","what":"Week 3, signed in, on an Auckland commercial site or the owner's own property. Shot A: the development-potential tiles. Shot B: the Rough feasibility card with homes prefilled. Shot C: typing illustrative land and sale prices so Profit and Margin appear. Shot D: raising the build cost until Margin turns red. Shot E: the green 'keep a 20% margin' box. No address bar."},{"kind":"kinetic","what":"An 'Illustrative numbers' tag on every money frame, count-up, red flash on the margin, the small-print card, captions and the end card."}],"cta":"Run your numbers before the room does. Site research is a free account at qsme.co.nz.","scores":{"recognizable":4,"specific":5,"ownable":4,"shareable":4},"why_now":"October and November are peak auction season in Auckland. The calculator shipped with the 30 September Site research rebuild.","risks":["Label every money figure 'Illustrative numbers'. The user supplies the land and sale prices.","Not financial or investment advice. QSME doesn't value land or know sale prices. The defaults are editable starting points, not QSME cost data.","Not a full feasibility: it leaves out GST, tax, holding period and sales commission. Say so on screen.","Auckland only: the homes figure exists only where an Auckland zone profile exists.","The filmed shot shows no faces, plates or real auction-house branding. The joke is auction FOMO, not bidders or agents."]},{"id":"nz-address-to-site-report","edition":"nz","title":"From an address to a site report","kind":"long-form","format":"long","length_s":155,"aspect":"16:9","platforms":["YouTube","Facebook"],"situation":"A first-time small developer in Auckland has three sections on a weekend shortlist and one Monday meeting with a planner she pays by the hour. She has to decide which address gets the planner's time, and walk in with the right questions and something on paper.","truth":"You pay professionals to answer questions, and most people don't know which questions to ask until after the deposit.","twist":"The tool's own last word is 'Confirm with a planner or the council', and the whole film builds to that line on purpose. QSME's job is to get you to the right questions and a one-page report to slide across the planner's desk, not to be the planner. Deadpan narrator, honest limits included.","hook":{"text":"Three sections. One planner. Which address gets the meeting?","verbal":"Three sections on the list, one planner on Monday. Here's how I picked.","visual":"A sticky note with three fictional street names on a laptop bezel. The cuts that follow cross them out one by one, ending on a printed one-page site report.","audio":"Marker squeaks, then the soft whirr of a printer."},"beats":["0:00–0:12 COLD OPEN: the sticky note (filmed or kinetic) and a chapter strip 'Address → Zone → Overlays → Services → Verdict → Requirements → Numbers → One pager'. CAPTION: 'Three sections. One planner.' VO: 'Three sections, one planner on Monday. Which one gets the meeting?'","0:12–0:30 'Search your address' on the public homepage (no sign-up): boundary, land area, legal description, council, zone, then the services and hazard chips. CAPTION: 'Step 1: the free check. Auckland first.' VO: 'Start on the Q-S-me homepage, no sign-up. In Auckland you get the zone, the pipes and the hazards.'","0:30–0:55 Site research page: progress line, parcel in cadastre yellow, 'What could go here' with 'Without resource consent' and 'With a resource consent', the standards grid (height, coverage, yards), then the Zoning layer washing the street in zone colours. CAPTION: 'What the zone allows, in plain words.' VO: 'Then Site research, on a free account. What the zone allows, in plain words.'","0:55–1:20 'Planning overlays & controls' grouped into Constraints / Design around / Opportunities / Good to know, then 'Site services & hazards' with pipes drawn and the dashed 'Distance to boundary' line. CAPTION: 'What touches this parcel. Where the pipes run, roughly.' VO: 'What touches the parcel, and where the pipes run, roughly. It even tells you to lodge a beforeUdig.'","1:20–1:45 The 'Development potential' verdict and tiles, then 'What this site would require' with the Required filter and its 'because:' lines, then a quick pass over 'Rough feasibility' with numbers tagged 'Illustrative'. CAPTION: 'A verdict. A to-do list with reasons. Rough numbers.' VO: 'A verdict, indicatively. What the site would need, and why. And a rough number, from my numbers.'","1:45–2:10 'Open Pro view': full-height aerial with the Zoning / Buildings / Services / Flood & hazards chips, the tab pills, and the Title tab showing '1 owner on the title' with the amber 'Available with a data licence' pill. Then the 'Saved sites' pills flick between the three shortlisted addresses. CAPTION: 'Three sites saved. Flick between them.' VO: 'Save all three and flick between them.'","2:10–2:25 'One pager', then the print preview of 'QSME · Site report' and Save as PDF. The printed page lands on a desk. CAPTION: 'One page to take to the planner.' VO: 'One page. That's what goes to the planner.'","2:25–2:35 CLOSE: the grey disclaimer 'Indicative only… Confirm with a planner or the council.' CAPTION: 'It even tells you to call them. Site research, free account. qsme.co.nz' VO: 'It even tells you to ring the planner. Fair enough. Q-S-me dot co dot en zed.'"],"features":[{"name":"Homepage address check (“Search your address”)","status":"live"},{"name":"Site research page (web, free account)","status":"live"},{"name":"Zone-first zoning: “What could go here” and the coloured zoning map","status":"live"},{"name":"Planning overlays & controls on the parcel (Auckland)","status":"live"},{"name":"Services & hazards snapshot (Auckland)","status":"live"},{"name":"Development potential verdict and indicative yield","status":"live"},{"name":"“What this site would require” checklist","status":"live"},{"name":"Rough feasibility calculator (“Your numbers, our arithmetic.”)","status":"live"},{"name":"Site research — Pro view (full map, layers, seven tabs, title facts)","status":"live"},{"name":"One-page site report (print or save as PDF)","status":"live"},{"name":"Saved sites, recent searches and research kept with the project","status":"live"}],"journey_stage":"Find a site → Feasibility","job":"organic","footage_plan":[{"kind":"capture-public","what":"Homepage check at 1920x1080 desktop on a public or commercial Auckland address (the same one used for 'The back corner')."},{"kind":"owner-recording","what":"Week 2, desktop 1920x1080, in the same session as 'Strong. Then I scrolled.', on three Auckland addresses that are commercial or the owner's own. Shot A: Site research loading and the parcel. Shot B: the 'What could go here' card and standards grid. Shot C: the Zoning layer toggle. Shot D: scrolling the overlays card. Shot E: the services and hazards card with pipes and the 'Distance to boundary' line. Shot F: verdict tiles. Shot G: the requirements card with the Required filter. Shot H: feasibility with illustrative inputs. Shot I: 'Open Pro view', the layer chips, tab pills and the Title tab. Shot J: switching between saved sites. Shot K: 'One pager' print preview and Save as PDF. Crop or blur address bars."},{"kind":"filmed","what":"Optional, hands only: the sticky note on a laptop bezel, and the printed one-pager landing on a desk."},{"kind":"clip-broll","what":"A 2 s stage-1-site clip as a chapter cutaway, labelled 'Illustrative'."},{"kind":"kinetic","what":"Chapter strip and numbers, sticky-note cross-outs, labels, burned-in captions inside 96,54,1824,1026, and the end card (body.nz r16x9)."}],"cta":"Start with your address. Site research is a free account at qsme.co.nz.","scores":{"recognizable":4,"specific":4,"ownable":5,"shareable":3},"why_now":"Site research was rebuilt 30 Sep–1 Oct, with the Pro view and one-pager added. A YouTube walkthrough catches evergreen searches like 'what can I build on my section Auckland', and it's the link every site-research short points to.","risks":["Overlays, services, hazards, the verdict and the requirements are Auckland only, and the captions say so.","Not a LIM, title search, valuation or advice. The one-pager isn't official or bank-ready. 'Pro' is a view name, not a paid plan.","The CV & sales tab is locked, so never mock values. Owner names aren't shown, only the count.","All three shortlisted addresses must be commercial or the owner's own. Blur anything residential.","It's rule-based public data, not AI. No competitor names. Say 'in seconds', not 'instant'."]},{"id":"nz-programme-survives-christmas","edition":"nz","title":"How a programme survives Christmas","kind":"long-form","format":"long","length_s":120,"aspect":"16:9","platforms":["YouTube","Facebook"],"situation":"Early October in Auckland: an owner-builder doing four townhouses has a spreadsheet programme that promises the buyer, and the lender, a mid-January handover. The spreadsheet works straight through Christmas Day, Boxing Day, New Year and Labour Day weekend.","truth":"Every NZ programme that crosses Christmas is about two weeks optimistic on paper, and everyone knows it except the spreadsheet.","twist":"The hero moment is bad news: '12 jobs are planned on days the site is shut', then 'Move them off the days off', and the finish date moves OUT. The film celebrates a later date as the most useful thing your software will do all year. Then it shows what makes that date hold: holidays, booked tradies being asked, the purple chain, and a printed programme you can still say out loud in January.","hook":{"text":"This programme works Christmas Day.","verbal":"This programme has the crew on site Christmas Day. And Boxing Day. And New Year's.","visual":"Kinetic: a generic spreadsheet Gantt with one bar sailing straight through 25 December to a 'Handover 15 Jan' flag. The 25 Dec column gets a red-marker circle.","audio":"A single jaunty sleigh-bell jingle that cuts off mid-shake."},"beats":["0:00–0:10 COLD OPEN: the kinetic spreadsheet. CAPTION: 'This programme works Christmas Day.' VO: 'This programme has the crew on site Christmas Day. And Boxing Day. And New Year's.'","0:10–0:30 The 'Townhouses' programme, put on from a start date with Days off set to 'This site works straight through Christmas and the New Year.' Bars run through late December and the FINISHES box reads mid-January. CAPTION: 'Same job in QSME, Christmas shutdown switched off.' VO: 'Same job in Q-S-me, with the Christmas shutdown switched off.'","0:30–0:50 Days off back on 'Christmas shutdown — Shut 23 December to 11 January, every year.' The amber '12 jobs are planned on days the site is shut' appears, then 'Move them off the days off', and the bars jump past 11 January as the finish moves out. CAPTION: 'Only 4 of those days are public holidays. Your spreadsheet called the other ~8 normal working days.' VO: 'Switch it back on. Twelve jobs sitting on days nobody's on site. Move them, and there's your real finish.'","0:50–1:05 'NZ public holidays built in': drop a task on Labour Day and it lands on the Tuesday, with a toast naming the holiday. Switch 'Works public holidays' on for the slab pour only. Then 'Add a day off' for Auckland Anniversary (not built in) and a 'Crew away' week. CAPTION: 'Labour Day, Waitangi, Matariki: built in. Auckland Anniversary: add it yourself.' VO: 'National holidays are built in. Anniversary Day isn't, so add it as your own day off.'","1:05–1:20 'Asked, not told': booked tradies whose dates moved see 'The site is shut then: …' and 'New dates — can you make them?'. 'Waiting for OK' chips show; one tradie confirms and the bar turns blue. CAPTION: 'Anyone booked is asked. Nothing changes for them till they say yes.' VO: 'Anyone booked gets asked, and nothing changes for them until they say yes.'","1:20–1:35 'What drives the finish date': purple rings join a chain. Drag a grey task and nothing happens; drag a purple one a day and the finish moves a day. CAPTION: 'Hurry a grey task: nothing. Shorten a purple one: earlier finish.' VO: 'Want January back? Don't hurry the grey ones. Only the purple ones move the finish.'","1:35–1:52 'As a PDF — the chart and the task list': the A4 'Construction programme' on letterhead with the FINISHES box, purple rings and 'Any one of them running late moves the whole finish.' A hand slides the printout across a desk. CAPTION: 'The date you give the buyer, on one page.' VO: 'Then print it. That's the date you give the buyer and the lender.'","1:52–2:00 CLOSE and CTA. CAPTION: 'A finish date you can still say out loud in January. Free to join. qsme.co.nz' VO: 'A finish date you can still say out loud in January. Q-S-me dot co dot en zed.'"],"features":[{"name":"Christmas shutdown and your own days off","status":"live"},{"name":"NZ public holidays built in","status":"live"},{"name":"Built-in NZ programmes (Townhouses, Standard NZ build, Land to titles)","status":"live"},{"name":"Asked, not told: booked tradies confirm new dates","status":"live"},{"name":"Linked tasks: one trade slips, everything after it moves","status":"live"},{"name":"What drives the finish date (the purple chain)","status":"live"},{"name":"Printed programme PDF (“Construction programme”)","status":"live"}],"journey_stage":"Schedule","job":"organic","footage_plan":[{"kind":"kinetic","what":"Spreadsheet cold open, a 23 Dec–11 Jan calendar overlay with public holidays marked, chapter cards, captions inside 96,54,1824,1026, and the end card (body.nz r16x9). Also cut a 30 s 9:16 'The January lie' from beats 1–3."},{"kind":"owner-recording","what":"Week 3, desktop. Shot A: Templates, 'Townhouses', pick a start date, put it on. Shot B: Days off set to 'This site works straight through Christmas and the New Year.' Shot C: switch to 'The usual 23 Dec – 11 Jan', the amber banner, 'Move them off the days off', and the FINISHES box before and after. Shot D: drop a task on Labour Day and show the toast. Shot E: 'Works public holidays' on the pour. Shot F: 'Add a day off' for Anniversary Day and 'Crew away'. Shot G: 'Waiting for OK' chips and a demo tradie confirming. Shot H: the 'What drives the finish date' toggle, dragging a grey task, then a purple one. Shot I: export 'As a PDF — the chart and the task list' with a demo letterhead set up."},{"kind":"clip-walkthrough","what":"client-schedule-deep.mp4, muted, for a generic order-of-work insert. It predates holidays and the shutdown, so use generic steps only and no 'Book a tradie' frames."},{"kind":"filmed","what":"Optional, hands only: sliding the printed A4 programme across a desk."},{"kind":"clip-broll","what":"th-4-structure and th-5b-lockup as 1–2 s chapter cutaways, labelled 'Illustrative'."}],"cta":"Put your programme on QSME before you promise January. Free to join at qsme.co.nz.","scores":{"recognizable":5,"specific":5,"ownable":4,"shareable":4},"why_now":"October is when programmes that cross Christmas get set and buyers get told dates. Labour Day (Mon 26 Oct) is the first long weekend that bites. The shutdown, holidays, purple chain in print and the programme PDF all shipped 22–23 Sep.","risks":["Old programmes only move when the user presses 'Move them off the days off'. Never imply they're fixed automatically.","Regional anniversary days aren't built in. Show adding one, and never claim 'every public holiday'.","Template durations are estimates. Don't voice the task count or the months as a promise.","Never say 'bank-approved' or 'bank-ready', or name a bank. Never use 'critical path' as a UI label. No percentages.","Tradie confirmations are in-app and email only. No SMS."]},{"id":"nz-pricing-a-build-line-by-line","edition":"nz","title":"Pricing a build line by line","kind":"long-form","format":"long","length_s":140,"aspect":"16:9","platforms":["YouTube","Facebook"],"situation":"A Christchurch owner-builder has a consent pack thick enough to prop a door open and a spreadsheet from her brother-in-law's build two years ago. She needs a budget she trusts, and quotes from trades that are pricing the same job, before the drainlayer prices from a pack with no drainage sheet in it.","truth":"Everyone prices a build from someone else's spreadsheet and a consent pack nobody has read cover to cover.","twist":"The best moment isn't a number, it's a gap: 'Heads up: no drainage found in this pack', found before the tender, not on site. And the estimate isn't the end of the job, it's the thing you tender. Every bidder prices the same lines and never sees your rates.","hook":{"text":"Found before the tender. Not on site.","verbal":"Before this went to the trades, Q-S-me told me what wasn't in it.","visual":"The plans-read notification 'Heads up: no drainage found in this pack.' slides in over a thick printed consent pack being dropped on a bench (filmed, hands only).","audio":"A heavy paper thud, then a single notification chime."},"beats":["0:00–0:10 COLD OPEN. CAPTION: 'Found before the tender. Not on site.' VO: 'Before this went to the trades, it told me what wasn't in it.'","0:10–0:30 'Read a document': 'Finding the sections…', then '4 of 11 sections ready', then chips like 'Floor plans p.4–7' and 'Bracing p.12–14'. Tap a chip and a window schedule appears as a real table. CAPTION: 'The pack, split into its real sections. (Paid plans; uses AI credits.)' VO: 'It splits the pack into its sections, like floor plans, bracing and schedules, and says what it didn't find.'","0:30–0:50 SOQ: 'Add a pocket', type a quantity and a rate, and the line, job, pocket and 'Grand total' tick up together. Add a 'Waste %' column and a formula. Then 'Import a schedule from Excel or CSV', 'We also found these columns', and it lands as drafts. CAPTION: 'Pockets, jobs, lines. The old spreadsheet comes in as drafts.' VO: 'Then the pricing. A pocket per trade, lines inside. The old spreadsheet comes in as drafts.'","0:50–1:10 'Draft SOQ with AI': pockets appear marked 'AI draft'. 'Keep' one and discard another. 'Estimate with AI' fills only the unpriced lines, each marked ✦. Edit one rate and its sparkle disappears, while the banner reads '3 lines priced by AI and not yet reviewed'. CAPTION: 'AI drafts. You check. Every AI number stays marked until you do.' VO: 'AI can draft the gaps, and every number it touched stays marked till I've checked it.'","1:10–1:25 'Chat with AI' with the Beta tag visible: 'What cladding is specified?' gets an answer labelled 'From your documents (Architectural set, p.12)'. CAPTION: 'Answers say where they came from. Beta: check before you price.' VO: 'Ask the plans and it tells you which page, or that it's guessing.'","1:25–1:50 'Create New Tender' from the Plumbing & drainage pocket: 'Select whole pocket', then 'Point them at the right sheets' with 'Drainage layout — p.22–24', a 30-30-40 payment split with 5% retention, and invites to contacts. CAPTION: 'The pocket becomes the tender. Each trade gets pointed at the right sheets.' VO: 'The pocket becomes the tender, and the drainlayer gets the drainage pages. The ones that exist now.'","1:50–2:10 Split screen: the owner's SOQ with rates beside the bidder's view of the same lines with the rate column empty ('Itemize'). Bids come back to the 'Compare bids' totals table with 'Lowest' and 'calc.', and one bid opens to its 'Detailed price breakdown'. CAPTION: 'Same lines for every bidder. Your rates never shown. Compared as totals; open any bid for its lines.' VO: 'Every bidder prices the same lines and never sees mine. Then the totals on the same GST basis, and each bid opens to its lines.'","2:10–2:20 CLOSE. CAPTION: 'Price it once. Tender the same lines. Free to join. qsme.co.nz' VO: 'Price it once. Tender the same lines. Q-S-me dot co dot en zed.'"],"features":[{"name":"Read my plans / Plan index: consent pack split into sections","status":"live"},{"name":"Schedule of Quantities (SOQ): pockets, jobs, lines, your own columns and formulas","status":"live"},{"name":"SOQ in and out: Excel/CSV import, Excel/CSV/PDF export, templates","status":"live"},{"name":"Draft SOQ with AI","status":"live"},{"name":"Estimate with AI (prices only the unpriced lines, flagged for review)","status":"live"},{"name":"Ask the plans: AI chat that labels where every number came from (Beta)","status":"live"},{"name":"Tender cut from an SOQ pocket: private or public, invitations and share link","status":"live"},{"name":"Tenders point each trade at the right sheets","status":"live"},{"name":"What a bidder sees and how they price (rates and budget never shown)","status":"live"},{"name":"Compare bids: totals excluding and including GST, with a 'Lowest' tag","status":"live"}],"journey_stage":"Estimate → Tender → Compare & award","job":"organic","footage_plan":[{"kind":"filmed","what":"Owner films a printed sample consent pack thudding onto a bench, hands only, with no title block legible."},{"kind":"clip-walkthrough","what":"client-soq.mp4, muted with the URL bar cropped, for SOQ basics. client-tenders.mp4, muted, for wizard steps only, cropping out the removed 'Quick Jobs' sidebar item. It predates the GST table, so don't use its comparison frames."},{"kind":"owner-recording","what":"Weeks 2–4, on a demo project with a sample plan set the owner has permission to show. Shot A: 'Read a document' and the 'Heads up' notification. Shot B: a chip opening to its table. Shot C: typing in the SOQ, then the Waste % column and a formula. Shot D: importing assets/qsme/samples/qsme-example-soq.xlsx. Shot E: Draft SOQ with AI, keeping one pocket and discarding one. Shot F: Estimate with AI, then a sparkle edit. Shot G: Chat with AI with the Beta line visible. Shot H: the tender wizard with page references, payment split and invites. Shot I: the bidder view on a second demo account. Shot J: the Compare bids table. Shot K: the Detailed price breakdown."},{"kind":"kinetic","what":"Chapter cards, the split-screen frame, a 'Paid plans; uses AI credits' tag, captions and the end card (body.nz r16x9)."}],"cta":"Price it once and tender the same lines. Free to join at qsme.co.nz.","scores":{"recognizable":4,"specific":4,"ownable":4,"shareable":3},"why_now":"'Read my plans' was hardened for real council packs in September, and 'Add a bid' and the GST comparison shipped late September. Owners pricing summer builds are searching YouTube for 'how to price a house build NZ' now. This film also absorbs the strongest parts of the 'Interrogating the plans' and 'Bathroom. Make nice.' cards.","risks":["'Line by line' in the title means pricing the SOQ. Never say or caption that bids are compared line by line.","The AI reads what the drawings say; it doesn't measure or do takeoff. Mark-up is switched off, so no 'Open drawing' buttons on screen.","Plan reading and the AI use credits on paid plans. Never call them free, and don't advertise the 14-day trial.","Keep the Beta tag and 'It can get things wrong' visible whenever the chat is on screen.","The plan set must be a sample or one the owner has permission to show; architects' title blocks are someone's IP.","QSME doesn't supply rates. AI estimates aren't quotes or market rates."]},{"id":"gl-day-23","edition":"global","title":"Day 23 of one signature","kind":"kinetic","format":"short","length_s":25,"aspect":"4:5","platforms":["LinkedIn","YouTube Shorts (9:16 re-export)"],"situation":"The ceilings subcontractor has been on site since Day 2. The subcontract went out 23 days ago. Since then: 'will sign tonight', a resend (it went to junk), a printer out of toner, and finally a sideways scan of pages 1–6 and 8–12. Page 7 is the retention clause.","truth":"The subcontract is the one document on the job that everybody agrees with and nobody has signed.","twist":"A deadpan day counter where every excuse is innocent and true, ending with a scan that skips exactly the page that matters.","hook":{"text":"Subcontract signature: Day 23.","verbal":"Day twenty-three of chasing one signature.","visual":"A big mono counter rolls from 01 to 23 over a scrolling stack of email replies.","audio":"Counter ticks, then one phone buzz"},"beats":["0–3s HOOK counter.","3–10s Message stack, each stamped with its day: Day 1 'Sent.' · Day 4 'Will sign tonight' · Day 9 'Can you resend? Went to junk' · Day 15 'Printer's out of toner' · Day 22 'Signed and scanned!' VO: 'Will sign tonight. Went to junk. Out of toner.'","10–13s Day 23: the scan arrives sideways, page strip '1 2 3 4 5 6 8 9…'. Caption: 'Page 7: retention.' VO: 'All there except page seven. Retention. Naturally.'","13–15s Caption: 'They've been on site since Day 2.'","15–21s PRODUCT (real QSME): our own subcontract PDF goes in via 'Sign it as it is', then 'Send for signing'. On a phone: tick 'I have read this agreement and I agree to sign it electronically.', type the name, draw, and 'Signed. Download a copy below and keep it.' Caption: 'One link. No account. Nothing to install.'","21–23s The sender's list flips from 'Out for signing' to 'Everyone has signed', with a flash of the certificate page. Caption: 'Every page. One signature.'","23–25s Caption: 'We still can't make them open it. We did make it one link.' Book a 30-minute demo, qsme.build."],"features":[{"name":"Agreements signed from a link — no account needed","status":"live"},{"name":"Bring in an agreement you already have, or have QSME write notes up as one","status":"live"}],"journey_stage":"Agree & sign","job":"organic-then-paid","footage_plan":[{"kind":"kinetic","what":"Day counter, stamped message stack, sideways page strip, captions, end card"},{"kind":"skit-ui","what":"Generic unbranded message bubbles and a generic PDF viewer showing pages 1–6 and 8–12"},{"kind":"owner-recording","what":"Week 2, demo Global org: our own subcontract PDF via 'Sign it as it is' → 'Send for signing' to an invented signer; the list moving to 'Everyone has signed'; the certificate page (invented signer, identifying fields cropped)"},{"kind":"filmed","what":"Owner, hands only: a phone in a dusty hand on a site table opening the signing link in the phone's browser, ticking, typing a name and drawing the signature"}],"cta":"Book a 30-minute demo, qsme.build","scores":{"recognizable":5,"specific":5,"ownable":3,"shareable":4},"why_now":"Link signing with a certificate page shipped 21–23 September and is free on every plan. 'Signatures chased for weeks' is in the brief, and using our own subcontract needs no NZ wording.","risks":["Built-in templates are written for NZ law. For Global use 'your own agreement' / 'Sign it as it is'. Never 'legally binding everywhere', 'court-proof' or legal advice.","No signing order, placed signature boxes, witnesses or initials exist.","Signing runs in the phone's browser, not the iOS app.","The certificate page shows network address and device, so use an invented signer and crop anything identifying.","The excuses stay innocent (junk folder, toner). The subcontractor is not the butt.","E-signing is common, so ownability rests on 'no account, nothing to install' plus bringing your own subcontract."]},{"id":"gl-chase-the-painter","edition":"global","title":"Action: chase painter","kind":"situation-demo","format":"short","length_s":30,"aspect":"4:5","platforms":["LinkedIn","YouTube Shorts (9:16 re-export)"],"situation":"Week 6 progress meeting. For six weeks the minutes have carried the same action: 'Chase painter (1 week late)'. The finish date moved anyway, because of something nobody minuted: a late switchboard holding up the electrical fit-off.","truth":"Most programme arguments are about the wrong delay. The loudest late trade usually isn't the one holding up the finish.","twist":"The minutes repeat one action six weeks running, and the self-own lands when the programme shows the painter was grey (had room) all along.","hook":{"text":"Week 6 minutes. Action: chase painter. (Again.)","verbal":"Six weeks we've chased the painter. The painter was never the problem.","visual":"A generic minutes document scrolls through weeks 1–6 with the same highlighted action line each week, and a pen underlines it a sixth time.","audio":"Pen click, page flip"},"beats":["0–3s HOOK.","3–7s Caption: 'Painter: 1 week late. Finish date: didn't move.' VO: 'Painter's a week late. The finish didn't move.'","7–10s A generic finish date ticks out 9 days. Caption: 'Something else moved it. Nobody minuted that.'","10–15s PRODUCT (real QSME): on the Gantt, tick 'What drives the finish date'. A chain of bars gets purple rings joined by thick arrows. Caption: 'Purple decides your finish date.'","15–19s Drag the painter's grey bar a week right and the last bar doesn't move. Caption: 'Grey has room to move.' VO: 'Shortening a grey task does nothing.'","19–23s Drag the purple electrical fit-off bar one day and the finish moves a day. Caption: 'Purple slips a day, the finish slips a day.' VO: 'Shorten a purple one.'","23–27s The printed A4 'Construction programme' slides across a desk; zoom on 'Any one of them running late moves the whole finish.' Caption: 'QSME calls it \"What drives the finish date\". You can call it the critical path.'","27–30s End card: Book a 30-minute demo, qsme.build."],"features":[{"name":"What drives the finish date (the purple chain)","status":"live"},{"name":"Linked tasks: one trade slips, everything after it moves","status":"live"},{"name":"Printed programme PDF (“Construction programme”)","status":"live"}],"journey_stage":"Schedule (programme reviews and progress meetings)","job":"organic-then-paid","footage_plan":[{"kind":"kinetic","what":"Minutes scroll, finish-date tick, captions, end card"},{"kind":"skit-ui","what":"Generic unbranded meeting-minutes document with six weeks of the same action line"},{"kind":"owner-recording","what":"Weeks 2–3, demo Global project with linked tasks, the Christmas shutdown switched off and no one booked on the dragged tasks: toggle 'What drives the finish date', drag a grey task (no change), drag a purple task (finish moves), export 'As a PDF — the chart and the task list'"},{"kind":"filmed","what":"Owner, hands only: the exported A4 'Construction programme' slid across a real desk, zooming on the plain-sentence line"},{"kind":"clip-walkthrough","what":"Week-1 fallback for linked tasks moving only: client-schedule-deep.mp4, muted, URL bar cropped, labelled 'product walkthrough (animation)'. It predates the purple chain, so it can't carry beats 4–7"}],"cta":"Book a 30-minute demo, qsme.build","scores":{"recognizable":5,"specific":5,"ownable":3,"shareable":4},"why_now":"The finish-date chain and the printed programme shipped 22–23 September. This is the brief's 'programmes nobody believes', and the payoff is literally on the printout.","risks":["Never put 'critical path' on screen as if it were the UI label, and show no float numbers or multi-path analysis.","The chain is off by default and only works on linked tasks, so set up the demo accordingly.","Switch off the default NZ Christmas shutdown (23 Dec–11 Jan) on the demo project. Global has no local public holidays.","A task with someone booked on it is sent for their OK rather than moved. Keep demo tasks unbooked, or show the amber 'waiting' state honestly.","No percentages, and hide money columns with GST labels.","Critical-path display is common in scheduling tools, so ownability rests on the plain-words printout.","The painter is innocent in this story; keep it a self-own by the meeting."]},{"id":"gl-as-above","edition":"global","title":"Wednesday: As above.","kind":"skit","format":"short","length_s":26,"aspect":"4:5","platforms":["LinkedIn","YouTube Shorts (9:16 re-export)"],"situation":"Friday 4:52pm in the site office. The site manager writes the whole week's diary from memory. Monday gets a paragraph. Tuesday: 'Works progressed.' Wednesday: 'As above.' Thursday: 'As above.' Friday: 'Ditto.' It rained on Tuesday until 11.","truth":"The site diary is written for the claim you hope never happens, at the moment you're least able to remember the week.","twist":"The entries shrink day by day down to 'Ditto'. Nine months later someone asks whether Tuesday 13 October was a rain day, and the diary says 'Works progressed.'","hook":{"text":"Site diary. Written Friday, 4:52pm. For Monday.","verbal":"Confession: my site diary gets written on Friday. Most of it says 'as above'.","visual":"Overhead phone shot: a hand in a hi-vis sleeve writing in a paper site diary, the entries visibly getting shorter down the page.","audio":"Pen scratch and a kettle clicking off"},"beats":["0–3s HOOK.","3–8s The week shrinks: MON (a full paragraph) → TUE 'Works progressed.' → WED 'As above.' → THU 'As above.' → FRI 'Ditto.' VO: 'Monday gets a paragraph. Wednesday gets as above.'","8–11s Card: 'Nine months later.' A generic email: 'Can you confirm whether Tue 13 Oct was a rain day?'","11–13s The diary's Tuesday: 'Works progressed.' Caption: 'It was. Probably.' VO: 'It was. I think.'","13–19s PRODUCT (real QSME): log a 'Rain day' for Tue 13 Oct on the programme and press 'Push these tasks'. The 'What happened' panel shows 'Rain day — Tue 13 Oct'. Cut to the Daily log: 'Rain day' and 'Schedule change' entries already sit under Tuesday. Caption: 'Log the rain day once. It writes itself into the diary.'","19–23s A chat photo is long-pressed, 'Mark as proof of work', and it appears in that day's log. Then 'Add a note for today…' and 'Add photo' on the phone. Caption: 'Photos land on the day you post them. Not on Friday.'","23–26s Caption: 'It doesn't write the whole diary. It writes the bits you'd forget by Friday.' Book a 30-minute demo, qsme.build."],"features":[{"name":"Daily log with photos (a site diary that partly writes itself)","status":"live"},{"name":"Rain days and the rain outlook","status":"live"},{"name":"Messages: project chat, direct and group, photos, files and voice notes","status":"live"},{"name":"What happened: full history, plain-words reasons, and Undo","status":"live"}],"journey_stage":"Build on site","job":"organic","footage_plan":[{"kind":"filmed","what":"Owner, hands only: a real paper site diary on a site-office desk with the five shrinking entries written in, hi-vis sleeve, no faces"},{"kind":"kinetic","what":"Handwriting reveal in Kalam/Caveat as a backup if filming slips; 'Nine months later' card; captions; end card"},{"kind":"skit-ui","what":"Generic email: 'Can you confirm whether Tue 13 Oct was a rain day?'"},{"kind":"owner-recording","what":"Week 3, demo Global project with weather-sensitive tasks, recorded on the web: the 'Rain day' dialog for Tue 13 Oct → 'Push these tasks'; the 'What happened' entry; the Daily log with auto 'Rain day' and 'Schedule change' entries; a chat photo marked 'Proof of work' appearing in the log; 'Add photo' on the phone"}],"cta":"Book a 30-minute demo, qsme.build","scores":{"recognizable":5,"specific":5,"ownable":3,"shareable":4},"why_now":"Rain days and schedule moves started writing themselves into the daily log in September. It's the brief's 'site diaries written on Friday for Monday', and Northern Hemisphere winter (rain-day season) is starting. Tue 13 Oct 2026 really is a Tuesday.","risks":["Only some entries write themselves (rain days, schedule moves, answers, checklist steps, documents, tagged chat). Never say 'it writes itself' without qualification.","A person logs the rain day and the forecast only shades the chart. Never imply automatic weather capture (some rival diaries do pull weather, which lowers ownability).","It is not a legal record or an extension-of-time tool. Photos are not GPS- or time-verified, and there is no video upload or offline mode.","Log the rain day on the web; phone rain-day logging is merged but not confirmed in the store app.","Keep it a first-person confession so the joke is on us, not on site managers."]},{"id":"gl-remittance-attached","edition":"global","title":"Remittance attached.","kind":"kinetic","format":"short","length_s":27,"aspect":"4:5","platforms":["LinkedIn","YouTube Shorts (9:16 re-export)"],"situation":"Month-end. The subcontractor's accounts team writes 'Claim 6 not received.' The main contractor's accounts team replies 'Paid on the 14th. Remittance attached.' There is no attachment. Both spreadsheets are right, according to themselves.","truth":"Half of payment admin is two people proving to each other whether money moved.","twist":"The thread escalates through every familiar reply ('Nothing attached?', 'Reattached', 'That's March's', 'Which account?') until someone says 'Can we jump on a call?'","hook":{"text":"'Remittance attached.' (It wasn't.)","verbal":"Remittance attached. ...It was not attached.","visual":"A generic email. The cursor scrolls to the bottom looking for a paperclip that isn't there, and finds empty white space.","audio":"Silence, then one lonely notification ping"},"beats":["0–3s HOOK.","3–9s The thread escalates one reply per beat: 'Nothing attached?' · 'Reattached' · 'That's March's' · 'Which account?' · 'Can we jump on a call?' VO: 'Reattached. That's March. Which account?'","9–12s Split screen: two generic spreadsheets, claim 6 reading 'PAID' on one and 'OUTSTANDING' on the other. Caption: 'Both correct. According to themselves.'","12–16s PRODUCT (real QSME): the contractor's milestone panel, 'Submit progress' with a site photo and their invoice.","16–21s The payer clicks 'I paid outside QSME', enters a 'Payment reference', ticks 'I have sent the money.' and presses 'Mark as paid', which shows 'Awaiting confirmation'. Caption: 'Only the payer can mark it paid.'","21–24s The receiver ticks 'The money has cleared in my account.', giving 'Settled outside QSME and confirmed by both sides.' A flash of the Project money page shows 'Cost paid' updating. Caption: 'Only the receiver can confirm it.'","24–27s Caption: 'QSME never touches the money. It keeps the one record you both ticked.' Book a 30-minute demo, qsme.build."],"features":[{"name":"Milestones: proof of work, invoice, then 'paid outside QSME' confirmed by the receiver","status":"live"},{"name":"Project money page: estimate vs actual, every stage, your own money in and out","status":"live"}],"journey_stage":"Pay","job":"organic-then-paid","footage_plan":[{"kind":"kinetic","what":"Email-thread escalation, captions, end card"},{"kind":"skit-ui","what":"Generic unbranded mail client and two generic spreadsheets with invented claim numbers"},{"kind":"owner-recording","what":"Week 3, demo Global contract with two separate demo accounts (payer and receiver): Stage 2 'Submit progress' with photo + invoice; payer 'I paid outside QSME' with reference and 'I have sent the money.'; receiver 'The money has cleared in my account.'; the final 'Settled outside QSME and confirmed by both sides.'; Project money 'Cost paid' updating"}],"cta":"Book a 30-minute demo, qsme.build","scores":{"recognizable":4,"specific":5,"ownable":4,"shareable":4},"why_now":"On Global, 'recorded, never moved' is the only way to settle, so the honest version is the whole story. Month-end claims come round every month, and no owner fact is pending.","risks":["Red list: never say 'secure payments', 'release the funds', escrow or payment protection. QSME doesn't verify that an outside payment happened.","Global has no bank button, and no Dispute button may appear on screen.","Never reuse payments.mp4 or awarded-work.mp4 audio or visuals ('release the funds', 'platform fee').","Hide currency and GST labels on the milestone panel.","The accounts teams on both sides are not the butt; the spreadsheets are."]},{"id":"gl-iso-27001-no","edition":"global","title":"Is QSME ISO 27001 certified? No.","kind":"kinetic","format":"short","length_s":32,"aspect":"4:5","platforms":["LinkedIn (founder profile + page repost)","YouTube Shorts (9:16 re-export)"],"situation":"A main contractor's procurement lead is on row one of a vendor security questionnaire ('ISO 27001 certified? Y/N'), and every software vendor so far has answered with a badge wall and a 'roadmap'.","truth":"Procurement can smell a badge that wasn't earned, and vague security answers stall software purchases for weeks.","twist":"The vendor's own website answers in one word, 'No.', then lists exactly what is in place and what isn't. Deadpan honesty is the pattern interrupt.","hook":{"text":"Is QSME ISO 27001 certified?","verbal":"Is Q-S-me ISO 27001 certified?… No.","visual":"Live capture of qsme.build/security: the cursor opens the FAQ 'Is QSME ISO 27001 certified?' and the answer reads 'No.'. Hold 1.5s on the word.","audio":"One click, then dead silence, with a single low tick on 'No.'"},"beats":["0–4s HOOK: the accordion opens to 'No.'","4–9s The next accordion, 'Do you have a SOC 2 report?', opens to 'No. We do not claim one.' Caption: 'Also no.'","9–15s Scroll to 'We do not display badges, logos or ratings we have not earned.' Caption: the same line, verbatim.","15–23s The 'What is in place today?' section. Highlight, verbatim: 'Rates, amounts and the tender budget are stripped out server-side, not hidden in the interface.' and the encrypted daily backups sentence. Caption: 'Here's what is in place.'","23–28s The programme status, verbatim: 'An information security management system aligned to ISO/IEC 27001:2022 is being built now.' Caption: 'In progress. Not certified. Said plainly.'","28–32s CTA: the page's own heading 'Need this in writing for procurement?' → end card 'Book a demo · qsme.build'."],"features":[{"name":"Honest security page (ISO 27001 in progress, not certified)","status":"live"},{"name":"Book a demo and the five audience pages (global)","status":"live"},{"name":"What a bidder sees and how they price (rates and budget never shown)","status":"live"}],"journey_stage":"12. Across every stage (decision: procurement and IT/risk reviewers at main contractors and developers)","job":"organic","footage_plan":[{"kind":"capture-public","what":"qsme.build/security scripted with capture.mjs: two accordion clicks, a scroll to the badges line, the 'In place' section and the programme status. Optional 1s tag of the same FAQ on /de/security or /ar/security, showing whatever that page actually says."},{"kind":"kinetic","what":"Deadpan type treatment (body.build) using verbatim quotes only, burned-in captions and end card. Kiwi VO script for later."}],"cta":"Need it in writing for procurement? Book a demo at qsme.build.","scores":{"recognizable":3,"specific":4,"ownable":5,"shareable":4},"why_now":"The security page is live and quotable word for word, and it answers the first question enterprise buyers ask. It's week-1 producible with no signed-in footage.","risks":["Needs owner sign-off: leading with 'No' is deliberate, but it may worry some enterprise buyers.","Quote the page exactly and never paraphrase upward ('certified soon', 'by 2027', 'basically compliant'). No badges, and no SSO, MFA, GDPR, pen-test or data-residency claims.","Backups are only 'merged': repeat the page's own sentence and say nothing about nightly runs or past incidents.","The hosting region is 'confirmed with sales', so don't name one."]},{"id":"gl-why-bids-never-compare","edition":"global","title":"Why bids never compare (and how to make them)","kind":"long-form","format":"long","length_s":125,"aspect":"16:9","platforms":["YouTube","LinkedIn (native video, founder profile)"],"situation":"A QS has five bids on a structure package and can't hold any two against each other. One priced drawing revision B and one revision C; one is 'plus tax'; one missed Tuesday's scope change; one arrived as an emailed PDF.","truth":"Bids don't fail to compare because subcontractors are sloppy. They fail because the tender let everyone price something slightly different.","twist":"Fix the tender, not the bidders: five causes, five mechanisms. The honest end is that QSME lines up the totals and opens each bid's own lines, but a person still reads them and decides. This is the long-form follow-up to 'Six bids. Six formats.'","hook":{"text":"5 bids. 0 that compare. It's not the subcontractors. It's the tender.","verbal":"Five bids, and not one of them compares. And that's not on the subcontractors. It's on the tender.","visual":"The six-bids props (sideways PDF, merged-cells laptop, whiteboard photo) are slid into a pile, and a hand lays one sheet titled 'Tender' on top.","audio":"A paper slide, then one pen click."},"beats":["0–10s HOOK, then title card: 'Five reasons bids don't compare.'","10–28s 1 · 'Everyone read the scope differently.' A tender cut from an SOQ pocket turns jobs and lines into the scope, so every bidder prices the same lines (bidder view: 'Est Qty / Bid Qty / Rate'). Caption: 'Same lines for every bidder.'","28–46s 2 · 'Two drawing revisions in circulation.' 'Point them at the right sheets': bidders see 'Referenced drawing pages', and when a new version is uploaded the reference warns 'pages may have moved'. Caption: 'Pages pinned to the version.'","46–62s 3 · 'The scope changed on Tuesday; one bidder never heard.' Editing an open tender shows '2 bid(s) are in on this scope. Change it and everyone still in the running is told what moved.' Caption: 'Change it once. Everyone still in is told.'","62–82s 4 · 'Plus tax, incl. tax, not registered.' Kinetic example labelled 'example, VAT 20%': 10,000 + VAT vs 11,500 incl. VAT becomes 12,000 vs 11,500 on the same basis, and the cheapest flips. Then the product: the tax basis is fixed onto each bid, 'Compare on' flips, and the 'Lowest' tag moves. Caption: 'Same tax basis, or it isn't a comparison.'","82–98s 5 · 'It came by email.' 'Add a bid you received': the PDF is read into a draft beside the original, a person checks it, and it appears as 'Received by you' in the same table. Caption: 'Emailed quotes go in the same table, after you check them.'","98–112s THE HONEST BIT: click a row to open 'Detailed price breakdown'. Caption: 'Totals side by side. Each bid's own lines one click away. You decide.' VO (Kiwi, later): 'It won't pick the winner for you. That's still your job. It's just a fair fight now.'","112–125s CTA, using the demo page's own offer: 'Bring a real package. We will cut the tender, invite two test bidders and walk through the comparison and award on the call.' · qsme.build. Comment prompt: 'Which of the five gets you most?'"],"features":[{"name":"Tender cut from an SOQ pocket: private or public, invitations and share link","status":"live"},{"name":"Tenders point each trade at the right sheets","status":"live"},{"name":"Read my plans / Plan index: consent pack split into sections","status":"live"},{"name":"What a bidder sees and how they price (rates and budget never shown)","status":"live"},{"name":"Compare bids: totals excluding and including GST, with a 'Lowest' tag","status":"live"},{"name":"Add a bid you received: emailed quotes join the comparison","status":"live"}],"journey_stage":"4. Tender → 5. Compare & award (consideration: QS/PM practices, main contractors). Follow-up for people who engaged with 'Six bids. Six formats.'","job":"organic-then-paid","footage_plan":[{"kind":"kinetic","what":"Five numbered reason cards, the VAT example (labelled 'example figures'), the comment prompt, burned-in captions and end card. For week 1, the hook is recreated from the six-bids motion source in projects/2026-10-02-qsme-build-six-bids-six-formats/motion-src. Kiwi VO script for later, ideally recorded by the founder (a QS) in his own voice."},{"kind":"filmed","what":"OWNER, optional, only if the six-bids prop shoot happens: a hands-only overhead of the printed props being pushed into a pile and the 'Tender' sheet laid on top."},{"kind":"owner-recording","what":"OWNER, weeks 2–4, in a demo global org: tender wizard 'Select whole pocket'; the bidder view; 'Point them at the right sheets' and the bidder's 'Referenced drawing pages', plus 'pages may have moved' after uploading a new version; the message when editing an open tender; 'Add a bid you received'; the 'Compare on' flip (tax header cropped); 'Detailed price breakdown'."},{"kind":"capture-public","what":"The qsme.build/pricing row 'Bidders pay to bid on your invitations — Never' as a supporting card."}],"cta":"Bring a real package to a 30-minute demo at qsme.build. We'll cut the tender and walk through the comparison with you.","scores":{"recognizable":5,"specific":5,"ownable":4,"shareable":4},"why_now":"Part 1 ('Six bids. Six formats.') sets up the problem, and all five fixes are live: the tax-basis table (21–23 Sep), emailed bids (19–20 Sep), drawing-page references and scope-change notices.","risks":["Never say or show 'compared line by line' or a bidder-by-line matrix. qsme.build's hero and /platform/tendering say it and show a matrix mock, so never capture either.","Never say 'conforming'. QSME doesn't judge compliance or pick the winner, and 'Lowest' is a tag, not a recommendation.","Comparison labels still read GST for global orgs, and the 'calc.' note says 15%: crop them and never say GST. The VAT card is our own arithmetic and clearly labelled as an example.","Drawing references are plain rows; the drawing viewer and mark-up are switched off.","The subcontractors stay the heroes ('not on them')."]},{"id":"gl-a-programme-a-lender-can-read","edition":"global","title":"A programme a lender can read","kind":"long-form","format":"long","length_s":122,"aspect":"16:9","platforms":["YouTube","LinkedIn (native video, founder profile)"],"situation":"The developer's funder wants an updated programme before the next drawdown review. The PM sends a Gantt screenshot that runs off the page, has no dates on the bars, and repeats one 450,000 contract on six rows, so the cost line reads 2.7 million.","truth":"The one question a lender asks, 'what is actually holding this build up?', usually has no answer on the page.","twist":"QSME prints a programme a non-builder can read. Every bar has its dates in words, the chain that sets the finish is ringed in purple with a sentence that says so, each contract's money counts once, and estimates are marked '~'. It goes out on your letterhead, and it never pretends a bank approved it.","hook":{"text":"Your programme. As the lender sees it.","verbal":"This is your programme. And this is what the lender sees.","visual":"A Gantt screenshot zooms out until the bars become unlabelled coloured sticks running off an A4 page. Six identical '450,000' rows add up to a highlighted '2,700,000' (tagged 'example figures').","audio":"A printer whirr, then paper landing on a desk."},"beats":["0–10s HOOK, then chapter card: 'Six fixes, one A4 page.'","10–26s 1 · FROM THE ESTIMATE: 'Import jobs from SOQ — The jobs you've priced on this project' → 'The dates below are what you'll get.' → 'Import 23 tasks', and the bars chain themselves. VO (Kiwi, later): 'Start from what you priced. Each job brings its own days.'","26–46s 2 · SLIPS AND RAIN: drag a task and the toast reads '3 moved · 2 sent for OK — 5 that follow them came too · Undo'. A booked subcontractor's task waits: 'This needs [name]'s OK — nothing changes for them until they say yes.' Log a rain day and see 'What moves'. VO: 'One slip moves what's linked. Anyone booked is asked, not told.'","46–64s 3 · WHAT DRIVES THE FINISH: switch on the purple chain. Drag a grey task and nothing moves; drag a purple one and the finish moves. VO: 'Purple decides the finish date. Grey has room.'","64–78s 4 · YOUR DAYS OFF: 'Add a day off' for the site's own holidays and shutdown → '12 jobs are planned on days the site is shut' → 'Move them off the days off'. Work moves later, never earlier. VO: 'Public holidays where you are? Add them once as days off.'","78–96s 5 · MONEY, ONCE: tick Cost and Paid. Tender-stage tasks show a grey '~' estimate and awarded ones a solid price, with 'Contracted · Paid · Still to contract ~'. VO: 'Estimates carry a squiggle. Agreed prices don't. And a contract counts once, not six times.'","96–114s 6 · PRINT: 'As a PDF — the chart and the task list' produces an A4 landscape 'Construction programme' on the developer's letterhead, with the boxes 'ON SITE FROM · FINISHES · HOW LONG · TASKS · NEXT MILESTONE' and the sentence 'Any one of them running late moves the whole finish.' Hands slide the printed page across a desk and a finger traces the purple rings.","114–122s CTA: 'Bring your programme spreadsheet to a 30-minute demo · qsme.build'."],"features":[{"name":"Fill the schedule from the estimate (SOQ import)","status":"live"},{"name":"Linked tasks: one trade slips, everything after it moves","status":"live"},{"name":"Asked, not told: booked tradies confirm new dates","status":"live"},{"name":"Rain days and the rain outlook","status":"live"},{"name":"What drives the finish date (the purple chain)","status":"live"},{"name":"Christmas shutdown and your own days off","status":"live"},{"name":"Money on the programme: Cost and Paid columns, estimate vs agreed","status":"live"},{"name":"Printed programme PDF (“Construction programme”)","status":"live"},{"name":"Everything you send goes out under your logo, not QSME's","status":"live"}],"journey_stage":"7. Schedule (consideration: developers/owners, PMs, main contractors)","job":"organic","footage_plan":[{"kind":"kinetic","what":"Hook with example figures tagged, chapter cards, burned-in captions and end card. Kiwi VO script for later."},{"kind":"owner-recording","what":"OWNER, weeks 2–4: a demo global project (non-NZ country, non-NZD currency, default Christmas shutdown switched off, demo letterhead set up). Record: the SOQ import; a drag with its toast; 'Send for their OK' plus the second demo account's 'New dates — can you make them?'; the rain-day 'What moves' preview; the purple chain; the days-off flow; the Cost/Paid columns; the PDF export."},{"kind":"filmed","what":"OWNER: the exported A4 'Construction programme' printed and slid across a real desk, hands only, a finger tracing the purple rings. The lender is fictional and never on camera."},{"kind":"clip-walkthrough","what":"Week-1 animatic: client-schedule-deep.mp4 (SOQ import, slab-cure gap, rain day, loop refused), labelled 'Illustrative walkthrough'. Mute the VO, crop the URL bar, and avoid frames showing 'standard New Zealand build' or 'Book a tradie'."},{"kind":"clip-broll","what":"stage-3-frame.v1.mp4 and th-5-lockup.v1.mp4 as cutaways (AI b-roll, never a real project)."}],"cta":"Bring your programme spreadsheet to a 30-minute demo at qsme.build.","scores":{"recognizable":4,"specific":5,"ownable":4,"shareable":3},"why_now":"The printed 'Construction programme', the purple chain in print and the money-counted-once rule shipped 22–23 Sep. No sample PDF exists anywhere yet, so this shoot also produces one for sales.","risks":["Never say 'bank-approved', 'bank-ready' or 'accepted by lenders', and never name a lender. The reader is fictional and hands-only.","The only built-in public-holiday calendar isn't the viewer's, so frame holidays strictly as 'add your own days off'. Switch off the default shutdown in the demo org.","Never use 'critical path' as a UI word. No percent-complete, cash-flow forecasting or float.","Asked-not-told works in-app and by email only, with no SMS. Keep native phone schedule shots out, because they aren't confirmed in the store app.","Money columns are visible to owners and managers only. The letterhead must be set up first; there is no logo by default."]}]}
